\subsection{Review Source}
\label{sec:review-source}

When using LLM-assisted review, we track where each decision originated.
This enables:
\begin{description}
\item[Learning from corrections] When a user corrects an LLM decision, that
  paper becomes a training example for future LLM rounds.
\item[Progress tracking] Users can see how many papers were reviewed by humans
  versus the LLM, and how many LLM decisions are pending review.
\item[Confidence prioritization] LLM decisions with low confidence can be
  prioritized for human review.
\end{description}

Machines decide in two ways, and a report must keep them apart.  An
LLM classifies papers through [[scholar llm classify]], one prompt per
batch, with a confidence per paper.  An AI \emph{agent} (an assistant
driving Scholar from the command line on the user's behalf) reads the
hits and records its own verdicts with [[sessions decide --source
agent]].  Both are machine decisions that no human has looked at, and
both used to be recorded as something else: an agent had to choose
between [[human]], which made the audit table cite its keeps as if the
author had read them, and [[llm]], which credited a classifier run that
never happened (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/162}).

So each machine source comes in two states.  The unreviewed one is what
the machine recorded; the reviewed one is what a human's confirmation
(or correction) turns it into.  The reviewed state keeps the machine's
name, because \enquote{a human confirmed what the agent proposed} is a
different fact from \enquote{a human decided}: the first says the
screening was delegated and checked, the second that it was not
delegated at all.  Five values therefore cover every decision Scholar
records.

<<review data structures>>=
class ReviewSource(Enum):
    """Source of a review decision."""
    HUMAN = "human"              # User decided directly
    LLM_UNREVIEWED = "llm"       # LLM decided, not yet reviewed by user
    LLM_REVIEWED = "llm_reviewed"  # LLM decided, user confirmed or changed
    AGENT_UNREVIEWED = "agent"   # AI agent decided, not yet reviewed
    AGENT_REVIEWED = "agent_reviewed"  # agent decided, user confirmed/changed

    <<unknown review sources>>
    <<review source properties>>
@

A session file can hold a source Scholar never wrote.  Real sessions
contain decisions with the source [[manual]], a value no version of
Scholar has produced (no commit in the history has it as a string, so
there is no legacy meaning to map it to), and a hand-edited [[Agent]]
would be another.  Loading used to turn every such value into
[[human]], so a decision of unknown origin became a citable human
decision: the report claimed more than is known about who decided
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/162}).

An unknown value is therefore kept as it is.  [[_missing_]] is the hook
[[Enum]] calls when a value matches no member; instead of failing it
returns a pseudo-member that carries the stored value, so the value
goes back to the file unchanged on save, and the same value always
gives the same object (it is cached in the enum's own value map, as
[[enum.Flag]] does for its combinations).  The pseudo-members are not
listed when the enum is iterated.  The properties below treat them as
unreviewed: never cited, queued for review, and turned into a human
decision when a human reviews them, since then a human has decided.

<<unknown review sources>>=
@classmethod
def _missing_(cls, value):
    """Keep an unknown stored value as a pseudo-member (`is_unknown`)."""
    if not isinstance(value, str):
        return None
    pseudo = object.__new__(cls)
    pseudo._value_ = value
    pseudo._name_ = "UNKNOWN"
    return cls._value2member_map_.setdefault(value, pseudo)

@property
def is_unknown(self) -> bool:
    """True for a stored value that is none of the known sources."""
    return self not in type(self).__members__.values()
@

Most readers of a decision ask one of three questions, and the
properties answer them so that no caller lists the members itself (a
list that forgets the agent is how a sixth source would slip into the
\enquote{cited} column).  [[is_unreviewed]] asks whether the decision
still waits for a human: such a decision is queued for confirmation in
the TUI and can never be cited.  [[is_machine]] asks whether a machine
proposed it, reviewed or not, so that the report can say how much of
the screening was delegated.  [[reviewed]] is the state a human review
moves a decision to; a decision that needs no review maps to itself,
and one of unknown source becomes a human decision.

<<review source properties>>=
@property
def is_unreviewed(self) -> bool:
    """True for a decision that waits for a human: an unreviewed machine
    decision, or one of unknown source."""
    return self.is_unknown or self in (
        ReviewSource.LLM_UNREVIEWED, ReviewSource.AGENT_UNREVIEWED,
    )

@property
def is_machine(self) -> bool:
    """True if an LLM or an agent proposed the decision, reviewed or not."""
    return self in (
        ReviewSource.LLM_UNREVIEWED, ReviewSource.LLM_REVIEWED,
        ReviewSource.AGENT_UNREVIEWED, ReviewSource.AGENT_REVIEWED,
    )

@property
def reviewed(self) -> "ReviewSource":
    """The source of this decision once a human has reviewed it."""
    if self.is_unknown:
        return ReviewSource.HUMAN
    return {
        ReviewSource.LLM_UNREVIEWED: ReviewSource.LLM_REVIEWED,
        ReviewSource.AGENT_UNREVIEWED: ReviewSource.AGENT_REVIEWED,
    }.get(self, self)
@

Reports count decisions by source (issue~\#163): a review whose 84 of
93 decisions are unreviewed LLM output reads very differently from one
a person screened, and the report used to show both as \enquote{93
papers (9 kept, 84 discarded)}.  The labels below are the single
wording of the five sources, in the order reports list them: the human
decisions first, then the machine decisions a human reviewed, then the
unreviewed ones, which are the ones a reader must discount.  Unknown
sources come last, each named by its stored value.  Only decided papers
are counted, since a pending paper carries the default source [[human]]
without anyone having decided anything.

<<review data structures>>=
DECISION_SOURCE_LABELS: dict[ReviewSource, str] = {
    ReviewSource.HUMAN: "human",
    ReviewSource.LLM_REVIEWED: "LLM, reviewed by a human",
    ReviewSource.AGENT_REVIEWED: "agent, reviewed by a human",
    ReviewSource.LLM_UNREVIEWED: "LLM, unreviewed",
    ReviewSource.AGENT_UNREVIEWED: "agent, unreviewed",
}


def source_label(source: ReviewSource) -> str:
    """The report wording of `source`, naming an unknown stored value."""
    if source.is_unknown:
        return f"unknown source '{source.value}'"
    return DECISION_SOURCE_LABELS[source]


def decision_source_counts(decisions) -> dict[ReviewSource, int]:
    """Count the decided papers in `decisions` by source, in report order.

    The known sources are always present; unknown ones follow, sorted by
    their stored value.
    """
    counts = {source: 0 for source in DECISION_SOURCE_LABELS}
    unknown: dict[ReviewSource, int] = {}
    for decision in decisions:
        if decision.is_decided:
            bucket = unknown if decision.source.is_unknown else counts
            bucket[decision.source] = bucket.get(decision.source, 0) + 1
    for source in sorted(unknown, key=lambda s: s.value):
        counts[source] = unknown[source]
    return counts


def decision_source_items(decisions) -> list[tuple[str, int]]:
    """Return `(label, count)` for each source that decided a paper.

    Sources without decisions are left out; the human row is always
    there when anything was decided, so that "0 human" is said aloud.
    """
    counts = decision_source_counts(decisions)
    if not any(counts.values()):
        return []
    return [
        (source_label(source), count)
        for source, count in counts.items()
        if count or source is ReviewSource.HUMAN
    ]


def decision_sources_sentence(decisions) -> str:
    """One sentence stating who decided, e.g. for an abstract.

    Empty when nothing is decided. Labels contain commas, so the parts
    are separated by semicolons.
    """
    items = decision_source_items(decisions)
    if not items:
        return ""
    parts = "; ".join(f"{count} {label}" for label, count in items)
    return f"Decisions by source: {parts}."
@

A decision recorded by [[sessions decide --source agent]] or [[--source
llm]] replaces whatever was decided before, and a classifier run may
have stored fields saying how it decided (the model, the prompt, the
reason).  Left in place, they would describe a decision that no longer
exists, and a report would attribute the agent's verdict to that model
run.  [[clear_machine_audit]] forgets them; it names no field itself
but reads [[MACHINE_AUDIT_FIELDS]], so the code that adds such a field
registers it there and every replacing path clears it.  The classifier's
confidence is not among them: [[sessions decide]] keeps or replaces it
deliberately (\cref{sec:sessions-decide}).

<<review data structures>>=
MACHINE_AUDIT_FIELDS: tuple[str, ...] = ()


def clear_machine_audit(decision) -> None:
    """Forget the audit fields a classifier run stored on `decision`."""
    for name in MACHINE_AUDIT_FIELDS:
        setattr(decision, name, None)
@

The report's paper lists name the unreviewed machine decisions where
they stand, not only in the counts, so that a reader who looks up one
paper learns that nobody has checked it.  The mark is the same label.

<<review data structures>>=
def unreviewed_mark(decision) -> str:
    """Label of an unreviewed or unknown-source decision, or "" otherwise."""
    if decision.source.is_unreviewed:
        return source_label(decision.source)
    return ""
