\subsection{What the LLM classifier recorded}
\label{sec:llm-audit-fields}

A decision that [[scholar llm classify]] made used to keep only its
source and the model's confidence.  The model, the date, the prompt and
the model's own reason were logged and then lost, so a report could say
that \enquote{an LLM} screened the papers but not which one, when, or
under which instructions---and a report that says less than happened
invites the reader to assume more.  So each decision now keeps what the
classifier knew when it decided:
\begin{description}
\item[ [[llm_model]] ] the model id the [[llm]] package reports, so two
  rounds with different models can be told apart;
\item[ [[llm_decided_at]] ] when the batch was answered, in UTC (ISO 8601),
  since a session may be classified over several days and on more than
  one machine;
\item[ [[llm_prompt_version]] ] a short hash of the prompt template and the
  options of the run (\cref{sec:prompt-version}), so that decisions made
  under the same procedure share a version and a changed procedure shows
  up as a new one;
\item[ [[llm_reason]] ] the model's stated reason, truncated, which is what a
  reviewer reads first when checking a doubtful row;
\item[ [[llm_abstract_truncated]] ] whether the model saw only the start of
  the abstract, because a decision made on a cut-off abstract rests on
  less than the reader would assume.
\end{description}
All five default to [[None]], which means \emph{unknown}: decisions
made before these fields existed, human decisions, and decisions
recorded with [[sessions decide --source llm]] on a paper the classifier
never decided.  None of them is
ever filled in with a guess.  The fields describe the machine's
decision, so a human who later confirms or changes it leaves them as
they are: they are the record of what the model said, which is what a
comparison of the two needs (\cref{sec:llm-sample}).

<<LLM audit fields of a decision>>=
llm_model: str | None = None
llm_decided_at: str | None = None
llm_prompt_version: str | None = None
llm_reason: str | None = None
llm_abstract_truncated: bool | None = None
@

The fields travel together everywhere a decision is copied, saved,
loaded or merged.  Naming them once keeps those places from drifting
apart: a sixth field added to the dataclass but forgotten in one copy
would be silently dropped the next time the session is appended to.

<<review data structures>>=
LLM_AUDIT_FIELDS: tuple[str, ...] = (
    "llm_model",
    "llm_decided_at",
    "llm_prompt_version",
    "llm_reason",
    "llm_abstract_truncated",
)


def llm_audit_of(decision: ReviewDecision) -> dict:
    """Return the LLM audit fields of `decision`, by name."""
    return {name: getattr(decision, name) for name in LLM_AUDIT_FIELDS}
@

They describe how a classifier run decided, so a decision that replaces
the classifier's must forget them: we register them with
[[clear_machine_audit]] (\cref{sec:review-source}).

