\subsection{Samples of machine decisions drawn for review}
\label{sec:llm-sample-record}

An LLM's confidence is its own report, and nothing in a session says
how often its unreviewed decisions are wrong.  [[scholar llm sample]]
(\cref{sec:llm-sample} in the LLM review module) answers that by
drawing a seeded random sample of unreviewed machine decisions for a
human to review.  The sample has to be stored in the session, for two
reasons.  The rate can only be computed later, once the human has
reviewed the sample, and by then the sampled rows look like any other
reviewed row; and a reader of the report must be able to check that
the sample was drawn as described, which needs the seed, the criteria
and the drawn papers, not only the rate.

So an [[LLMSample]] keeps how it was drawn ([[seed]], [[criteria]],
[[drawn_at]] in UTC, the [[sessions]] pooled and the size and a digest
of the [[population]] drawn from) and, for the drawn papers of this
session, the machine's decision at the time: its status, source and
confidence.  Storing the status is what lets the report count a
\enquote{changed} decision without relying on a side effect of the
review, such as [[is_example]], which the TUI also sets for other
reasons.  One sample drawn over several sessions is stored in each of
them under the same [[sample_id]], each with its own papers, so that a
report on one session can still state the whole sample's size.

<<review data structures>>=
@dataclass
class LLMSample:
    """A seeded random sample of machine decisions drawn for human review.

    `papers` holds the drawn papers of this session only, each as
    `{"paper_id", "status", "source", "confidence"}` with the machine's
    decision when the sample was drawn; `size` and `population` are those
    of the whole sample, over all `sessions`.
    """
    sample_id: str
    seed: int
    drawn_at: str
    criteria: dict
    sessions: list[str]
    population: int
    population_digest: str
    size: int
    papers: list[dict] = field(default_factory=list)

    def to_dict(self) -> dict:
        """Return the sample as JSON-ready data."""
        return {
            "sample_id": self.sample_id,
            "seed": self.seed,
            "drawn_at": self.drawn_at,
            "criteria": dict(self.criteria),
            "sessions": list(self.sessions),
            "population": self.population,
            "population_digest": self.population_digest,
            "size": self.size,
            "papers": [dict(p) for p in self.papers],
        }

    @classmethod
    def from_dict(cls, data: dict) -> "LLMSample":
        """Rebuild a sample saved by `to_dict`."""
        return cls(
            sample_id=data["sample_id"],
            seed=data["seed"],
            drawn_at=data["drawn_at"],
            criteria=dict(data.get("criteria") or {}),
            sessions=list(data.get("sessions") or []),
            population=data.get("population", 0),
            population_digest=data.get("population_digest", ""),
            size=data.get("size", 0),
            papers=[dict(p) for p in data.get("papers") or []],
        )
@

A session lists its samples in [[llm_samples]], oldest first; sessions
saved before samples existed load with none.  Samples only accumulate,
like snowball rounds, so a concurrent save adds the samples that only
the file has (\cref{sec:session-integrity}).

<<load the LLM review samples>>=
session.llm_samples = [
    LLMSample.from_dict(sample) for sample in data.get("llm_samples") or []
]
