\subsection{Search Record}

To reconstruct the exact [[scholar search]] that surfaced a paper, a session
must remember not just the query sent to each provider but the \emph{filters}
and \emph{limit} applied alongside it.  These belong to the individual search,
not the session as a whole: a session accumulates searches across [[rq]]
re-runs and snowball rounds, and those can carry different filters, so a single
session-level filter set would misattribute one search's constraints to
another.  We therefore record one [[SearchRecord]] per [[(query, provider)]]
search.

A record must also say what the search \emph{returned}, or a session
cannot tell a query that matched nothing from one the provider never
answered---the gap that let a blocked DBLP leave zero hits across four
review sessions.  So the record keeps the outcome of the search as well:
[[hits]] is the number of papers it produced, [[error]] the reason it
failed (\cref{sec:search-result-error}), and [[retries]] how often it was
repeated before the answer was accepted.  Records written before these
fields existed load with [[hits]] unknown ([[None]]), no error and no
retries: we cannot reconstruct what an old search returned, and claiming
\enquote{answered} for it is no worse than what such a record has always
claimed.  The record also keeps the [[total]] the service reported, and
whether it was an estimate, so that a count cut at the limit reads as
one (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/166}); older records
load with the total unknown.  And it keeps when the search ran and when
its answer was fetched (\cref{sec:search-dates} in the [[scholar]]
module), which differ for an answer served from the cache, so that a
report can state the date each source was searched
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/167}).  Older records
load with both dates unknown, and the session's own [[timestamp]],
reset at every append, is no stand-in for them.

\label{sec:search-record-key}
A search is identified by its query, its provider \emph{and} its
filters.  The same words sent to OpenAlex with [[--year 2010-2019]] and
with [[--year 2020-2024]] are two searches, with their own papers and
their own hit counts.  When the key was only [[(query, provider)]], the
second run replaced the first one's record, and the first run's filters
and hits were lost although its papers stayed in the session
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/140}).  The filters enter
the key through [[filters_key]] (\cref{sec:filters-key} in the
[[scholar]] module), the canonical string of the stored dictionary,
because two dictionaries can describe the same filters in different
orders.  The limit is deliberately not part of the key: it decides how
many of a search's papers we fetch, not which search it is, and a re-run
with a larger limit should replace the earlier record of that search,
not stand beside it.

<<review data structures>>=
@dataclass
class SearchRecord:
    """One provider search: its query, filters, limit, and outcome.

    ``filters`` is the dict form of the ``SearchFilters`` that were applied
    (``None`` when none were, or when the record predates filter
    persistence); ``limit`` is the per-provider result cap.  ``hits`` is
    the number of papers the search produced (``None`` for records that
    predate it), ``error`` why the provider failed (``None`` when it
    answered), and ``retries`` how often the search was repeated.
    ``total`` is the number of matching records the service reported
    (``None`` when unknown), ``total_estimated`` whether it called that
    an estimate. ``searched_at`` is when the search ran, ``fetched_at``
    when its answer was fetched from the service (UTC ISO times;
    ``None`` when unknown) and ``from_cache`` whether the answer came
    from the cache (``None`` when unknown).
    ``via`` names the endpoint that answered when the provider has
    several (``None``: the provider's default, or not recorded).
    """
    query: str
    provider: str
    filters: dict | None = None
    limit: int | None = None
    hits: int | None = None
    error: str | None = None
    retries: int = 0
    total: int | None = None
    total_estimated: bool = False
    searched_at: str | None = None
    fetched_at: str | None = None
    from_cache: bool | None = None
    via: str | None = None

    @property
    def key(self) -> tuple[str, str, str]:
        """The `(query, provider, filters key)` identifying the search."""
        return (self.query, self.provider, filters_key(self.filters))

    @property
    def is_legacy(self) -> bool:
        """Whether the record predates stored filters, which are unknown.

        Such records have neither filters nor a limit; every search
        recorded since stores its limit.
        """
        return self.filters is None and self.limit is None

    <<search record properties>>
@

[[via]] names the endpoint that answered when a provider has more than
one (\cref{sec:search-result-via} in the scholar module): [[bulk]] for
a Semantic Scholar search that went to its bulk search (and
[[bulk, most cited first]] when it had a limit).
It is not part of the key: which endpoint a provider uses follows from
the query and the limit, so it does not tell two searches apart that
the key would merge.
Nor is it part of [[record_rank]], by which a session keeps one of two
records of a search (\cref{sec:wider-record}): a bulk run of a query
that the relevance search once answered is the same search, and the
newer or wider run replaces the older record like any other.
That is intended.  For a Boolean query the relevance answer was the
zero of \url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/161}, and for a
plain-word query the bulk record is the wider one, [[--limit 0]]
against a limit.
It is stored with the record and read back with [[rec.get]], so a
session saved before it existed loads with [[None]], which reads as
\enquote{not recorded}, never as a claim about the endpoint.

One kind of record cannot take part in that key.  Sessions written
before filters were stored (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/61},
June 2026) kept only the pairs of query and provider; loading them, or
records that later saves copied from them, gives records whose
[[filters]] is [[None]] because the filters are \emph{unknown}, not
because there were none.  Their key would claim an unfiltered search.
They can be told apart: the change that started storing filters
(commit [[1e02d34]], \#61) stored the limit with them, in the same new
[[SearchRecord]], and since \#55 (commit [[e478a05]], the same day)
[[Search.execute]] gives every result its limit, as [[rq]] does with
[[effective_limit]].  So a record from a search always has a limit,
and one with neither filters nor limit is a legacy record
([[is_legacy]]).  Code outside scholar that builds a [[SearchResult]]
without a limit, like the workaround in
\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/135}, makes records that
look legacy too; they are then treated as the older searches are.

A legacy record is handled as before filters joined the key: a new run
of its query on its provider replaces it, whatever the new run's
filters (\cref{sec:legacy-records}).  Keeping it beside the new record
would make [[found-via]] print, for it, a command without filters that
may never have been run.

\label{sec:merged-records}
A second kind of old record cannot be split either.  Until
\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/164} a multi-provider
[[search -n]] stored its merged result as one record: provider
[[openalex, dblp]], query [[q | q]], the deduplicated count of all
providers together and their errors joined, each prefixed with its
provider.  Searches now record one record per provider
(\cref{sec:review-found-by}), but sessions hold hundreds of the old
ones, and their papers link to them.  They load unchanged, since
rewriting them would invent per-provider counts that were never
stored.  What changes is how they are read: [[is_merged]] recognises
them by the comma that no provider name contains, and
[[search_outcome_label]] says what such a record is---\enquote{merged
record (providers openalex, dblp): outcome cannot be split}, with the
total and the provider-labelled errors after it---instead of a count
that a reader would take for each provider's.

<<search record properties>>=
@property
def merged_providers(self) -> list[str]:
    """The providers of a merged record, or `[provider]` for one search."""
    return [p.strip() for p in self.provider.split(",") if p.strip()]

@property
def is_merged(self) -> bool:
    """Whether the record is a merged one from before #164."""
    return len(self.merged_providers) > 1
