When the same query is searched again on the same provider with the
same filters, the new record wins: [[add_query_provider_pair]] keeps the
first record of a key, and the current results are added before the
previous session's.  A run with other filters is another search and
leaves the earlier record alone (\cref{sec:search-record-key}).
That is right when the re-run answered---it is the newer answer---but
wrong when it failed.  Re-running a query while a provider is down would
otherwise overwrite \enquote{12 hits} with \enquote{failed}, although the
twelve papers from the earlier run are still in the session.  So an
earlier record that answered replaces a failed re-run of the same pair.
The failure itself is not lost from view: it was reported when it
happened, and the session keeps the answer that its papers came from.

That holds for a re-run that failed \emph{outright}, with no papers.
A re-run that failed part-way, \enquote{incomplete}, did answer, in
part, and its papers are added to the session; it is the latest answer
of the search, and it replaces the earlier record with its own outcome,
marked incomplete.  Keeping the earlier record would have been wrong
twice: a Boolean query that the old relevance search answered with 0
and the bulk search now answers with five papers before it is cut
short would keep \enquote{0} beside five new decisions, and the date
the source was last searched would be the old one.  A wider earlier
record still wins over a narrower re-run (\cref{sec:wider-record}).

These rules, and the one for limits below (\cref{sec:wider-record}),
decide between two records of one search, and they are needed in two
places: here, between this run's record and the session's, and when a
save meets a record of the same search that another process saved
meanwhile (\cref{sec:session-integrity}).  Applied in one place only,
the other undid them: a process still holding the narrow record of
[[-l 5]] saved it over the [[-l 0]] record another process had just
saved, with fifty papers linked to it.  So the rules are one function,
[[preferred_record]], which both places call.

What the rules make of a session is this: the record of a search, its
query, provider and filters, is its \emph{latest answered search of the
widest scope}, and the record reports that search's own outcome, its
count, its error and its dates.

The function compares one sort key per record, [[record_rank]], rather
than going through cases.  A first version went through cases (an
answer over a failure, then the wider limit, then a partial answer
with more hits over an answer with fewer, then the later run), and
cases interact: an undated record tied with a dated one, so which
survived a concurrent save depended on which process saved last, and
three records could beat each other in a circle (ten hits on 18
November over a partial twenty on 10 November over thirty on
1 November over the first).  A key compared lexicographically cannot
do either: it orders all records, a total preorder, and the outcome of
a comparison does not depend on the order of the arguments, except
between records whose keys are equal.  The key is, in order of weight:
\begin{description}
\item[Answered] An answer, also a partial one, beats a failure
  outright ([[failed_outright]]).
\item[Width] The wider limit wins, no limit ([[None]] or 0) the widest
  (\cref{sec:wider-record}).  A legacy record's limit is unknown, not
  unlimited, so it ranks below every known limit: a re-run that
  answered replaces it, as the legacy rule says
  (\cref{sec:legacy-records}).
\item[Run] The later run wins, and a dated record beats an undated one.
\item[Hits] Of runs of the same date, or of undated records such as
  legacy ones, the one with more hits wins; an unknown count ranks
  lowest.
\item[Complete] Of equal counts, the one without an error wins.
\end{description}
The date ranks above the count because the session must say when each
source was last searched, which PRISMA asks for: an older complete
answer of thirty hits must not displace a newer complete one of ten,
or the session would date the search to the older run although it was
run again since.  The count cannot rank above the date for partial
answers and below it for complete ones either: any such mix makes the
circle above possible again, which no key can express.  So a newer
answer always replaces an older one of the same scope, complete or not,
and shows its own outcome; a newer partial answer reads
\enquote{incomplete}, which is what the latest search was.

The papers that older runs of the same search returned keep their
links: a link names the search by its key, query, provider and
filters (\cref{sec:search-record-key}), which the replacing record has
too, so they stay attached to the search's one record.  That record
may then count fewer hits than it has linked papers, when the older run
returned more; the papers were found by that search, and the count says
what its latest run returned.

Two records with equal keys are a true tie, and the first argument
wins, so the caller decides: the new run here, the process's own record
in a save.  Both callers go through [[session_preferred_record]], which
adds the one rule that needs the session's papers: a failed re-run
replaces a record without an outcome unless a paper links to it or
names its provider (\cref{sec:unknown-outcome-replaced}).

