\paragraph{A failed re-run and a record without an outcome}
\label{sec:unknown-outcome-replaced}

One pair of records needs more than the two records to decide.  A
record written before outcomes were kept has neither hits nor an error
([[outcome_unknown]], \cref{sec:unrecorded-outcome}), and
[[record_rank]] ranks it as answered, so a re-run of the same search
that failed outright never replaced it.  A session holding
[[SearchRecord("q", "dblp", None, 40)]] kept that record after a DBLP
re-run that got the anti-bot page (\enquote{non-JSON response}): the
failure was recorded nowhere, and the report went on saying
\enquote{Outcome not recorded: dblp}
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/190}).  Such a
session could never say that the provider fails now, short of editing
the file, although the session must say when each source was last
searched and with what result.

The reason for keeping an answered record over a failure is that the
session holds the answer's papers.  For a record without an outcome
that reason holds only if the session has papers that its run may have
returned; then replacing the record would turn a possible answer into a
failure, and the report would call the provider \enquote{not searched}
beside its papers.  Without such papers the record shows nothing that
the failure would hide; it only says that the query was once sent.  So
the failed re-run replaces it unless the session has evidence of an
answer, of either of two kinds.
\begin{description}
\item[A link] Some decision's [[found_by]] names the record
  (\cref{sec:review-found-by}): its key, or, for an old link, only its
  query and provider (filters key [[None]],
  \cref{sec:session-persistence}), since that link may mean this
  record.
\item[A paper from the provider] Some decision's [[provider]] names the
  record's provider, compared as [[decision_provider_names]] compares
  it (\cref{sec:unrecorded-outcome}).  Links alone do not suffice,
  because they are younger than outcomes: records without an outcome
  were written before links were kept
  (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/128}), so their papers
  almost never link to them.  A session searched on OpenAlex then, with
  a re-run refused for a spent daily budget, would otherwise read
  \enquote{Not searched: openalex} while it holds OpenAlex's papers.
  Decisions with links count too: a paper that the old run returned
  and a later run returned again carries only the later run's link, so
  its links do not show that the old run did not return it.
\end{description}
The second kind is evidence about the provider, not about the query,
so it keeps every record without an outcome of a provider the session
has papers from, also of a query that returned none of them; such a
record then stays \enquote{outcome not recorded}, which is what the
session can tell.  And it is no proof: before one record was kept per
provider search (\cref{sec:merged-records}), every paper of a merged
search was given every merged provider's name.  Since
[[decision_provider_names]] reads each decision through its evidence
([[decision_provider_evidence]], \cref{sec:provider-evidence}), such a
decision keeps DBLP's old record only when it has no evidence at all
(no sources, no single-provider link) or its evidence names DBLP; one
whose sources show that OpenAlex alone returned it no longer does.
Without either
kind, as in the issue's session, whose papers name OpenAlex only, the
DBLP failure replaces the old record.
A legacy record (\cref{sec:legacy-records}) has no outcome either, and
in [[create_review_session]] it meets a re-run of its query and
provider through the same call, so a failed re-run replaces a legacy
record without evidence too; the legacy rule then has no links to move.

Whether the session holds such evidence is a fact about the session,
not about the record, so the rule cannot be part of [[record_rank]]:
the rank is a key of one record, and it is what keeps
[[preferred_record]] a total preorder.  The rule is a second step
instead.  [[session_preferred_record]] asks [[preferred_record]] and
overrides its choice in this one case.  The override applies to a pair
of one record without an outcome and one failed outright, whichever
argument each is, and [[preferred_record]] picks the record without an
outcome in both orders; so the result still does not depend on the
order of the arguments.  Both places that call [[preferred_record]]
call this instead: [[create_review_session]] with the links and
provider names of the previous session (a failed run has no papers, so
it brings no evidence of its own), and the merge of a save
(\cref{sec:session-integrity}) with those of the merged decisions.
There the names come from both sides: for a paper that both hold, the
merge unites the links but keeps this process's [[provider]] field, so
the file's names would otherwise be lost, and a paper is evidence on
either side.
Applied in one place only, the other would undo it: a process that
loaded the session before the re-run would save the old record back
over the failure, and a process that did the re-run would get the old
record back from its own file.

