\label{sec:legacy-records}
A legacy record (\cref{sec:search-record-key}) is one search of its
query and provider with unknown filters.  When this run searched the
same query on the same provider, we do what was done before filters
joined the key: the new record replaces it, whatever its filters
(unless the new run failed and the legacy one answered, which the
rule above already settled the old way).  The comparison is with this
run's own records only ([[current_run]]); a record that an earlier
session added is no evidence about the legacy search.

The papers of the replaced search keep their links, which now point at
the record that replaced it.  That is what the session said before
filters joined the key: its one record of the pair stood for every run
of it.  [[replaced_links]] maps each replaced key to its successor, and
once all decisions are built every link is moved
([[<<point links at the records that replaced theirs>>]]); duplicates
that the move creates are merged, keeping the first.

\label{sec:wider-record}
A re-run with a smaller limit is the opposite case: it answered, but
asked for less.  The limit is not part of the key
(\cref{sec:search-record-key}), so a run with [[-l 5]] after one with
[[-l 0]] is the same search, and the newer record used to win.  The
session then said \enquote{limit 5, hits 5} for fifty linked papers,
and [[found-via]] built its command from that record, a command that
returns five of them (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/166}).
A narrower run of the same query, provider and filters fetches the
first part of what the wider one fetched, so the wider record already
accounts for its papers: we keep the wider record and record nothing
new.  The narrower run's papers link to the key they share with it, so
their links name the wider record without being moved.  (Should the
service have reordered its ranking in between, a paper of the narrower
run may not have been among the wider run's; re-run today, the wider
search returns it, which is what the link claims.)

\enquote{Wider} compares the limits, with no limit ([[None]], or 0)
the widest; [[-l 0]] is stored as the large internal cap and so is
wide already.  A legacy record's missing limit means \emph{unknown},
not unlimited, so a legacy record is never wider; it has its own rule
below.  A wider record that failed outright does not stand in for a
narrower answer; otherwise the wider record wins, also over a narrower
re-run that failed part-way with more papers than it counts (the wider
record then shows fewer hits than the papers linked to it, a known and
accepted case).  [[preferred_record]] above applies the rule.

<<review session functions>>=
def search_width(limit: int | None) -> float:
    """How many results a search with `limit` may fetch; no limit is all."""
    return float("inf") if not limit else float(limit)
