For papers in the current search results, we merge with any existing decision
data.
If the paper was previously reviewed, the decision is kept whole and its
stored paper is kept too, with its gaps filled from the copy the search
returned ([[stored_paper_with]]); the decision's provider gains the
search's provider ([[united_providers]]).
Until November 2026 the decision was instead rebuilt around the
returned copy, as \enquote{fresh paper data}
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/192}).  That copy is
only what one provider returned this time: Semantic Scholar's bulk
search returns many works without an abstract, so adding it to a
session emptied about 1280 stored abstracts that earlier providers and
enrichment had supplied, set their sources to [[s2]] alone, and named
[[s2]] as the only provider.  Screening criteria such as
\enquote{no abstract} then judged records that had had one.  The stored
paper is what the review saw; a re-run may add to it, never take away.
[[merge_with]] is the rule the duplicate branch below already uses:
the stored value wins where both have one, the other copy fills what is
missing, sources are united and the higher citation count is kept.  The
two have the same id here, so [[merge_with]] accepts them.  A paper
whose previous decision is found under its legacy hash id is the other
case: the stored copy has no DOI and the returned one has.  They may be
two versions of the work, a DBLP record of the conference paper and an
arXiv preprint, say, and giving the stored copy the returned DOI would
make an arXiv DOI with the conference's venue and year.  So that case
follows the rule the session already applies to such pairs
(\cref{sec:doi-less-copies}): the DOI copy keeps its identity and
version-specific fields and gains the stored abstract, keywords,
affiliations and sources ([[merge_version_of]]); little else is lost,
since a paper without a DOI holds no Crossref or Unpaywall results.
[[replace]] keeps the decision's fields, also those added after this
code; its lists are copied, so the decision does not share them with the
previous session's, which may stay in the session beside it.  The
other branches that meet a paper again (the duplicate, folded, renamed
and adopted ones) unite the provider too, so the audit rows name every
provider that returned it.

<<review session functions>>=
def stored_paper_with(stored: Paper, returned: Paper) -> Paper:
    """The stored paper, its gaps filled from a copy a search returned.

    Stored values win; see `Paper.merge_with`.  When the stored paper
    has no DOI and the returned copy has one, the two are versions of
    a work: the returned copy keeps its identity and gains the stored
    content (`merge_version_of`).
    """
    if not stored.doi and returned.doi:
        return merge_version_of(returned, stored)
    return stored.merge_with(returned)
