@

Since a paper can appear in multiple providers, we also deduplicate within the
current result set.
When we encounter a duplicate paper ID, we keep a single [[ReviewDecision]] and
merge the underlying [[Paper]] objects to consolidate metadata (e.g., a missing
abstract in one provider and a present abstract in another).

A session entry may also be an \emph{adopted preprint}
(\cref{preprint-module}): enrichment replaced the published paper with
its preprint, keeping the published DOI in [[published_versions]].
Adoption breaks the id-based matching in both directions, and each
direction needs its own guard:
\begin{description}
\item[Published returns after adoption] The session holds the adopted
  preprint; a re-run returns the published version under its old id,
  which matches no decision.  The provenance lookup below maps each
  recorded published DOI back to its adopted entry, so the returning
  published paper is folded into that entry (content gaps filled via
  [[merge_version_of]], which never disturbs the preprint's identity)
  instead of re-entering as a fresh pending paper.
\item[Adoption arrives after the published entry] The session holds
  the published entry from an earlier run; a re-run with enrichment
  now delivers the adopted preprint under a \emph{new} id.  Here the
  incoming paper itself carries the link: its [[published_versions]]
  DOI matches the existing decision, whose entry we upgrade to the
  preprint identity---preserving the decision status and tags, since
  the work under review is unchanged---and re-key.  The persistent
  note and decision stores are migrated the same way
  ([[migrate_paper_id]], \cref{notes-module}).
\end{description}

\label{sec:review-surname-first}
A third kind of entry changes identity without changing version.
Scopus writes names surname first (\enquote{Fares E.}), and until the
provider learnt to reorder them (\cref{sec:utils-surname-first}) a
Scopus paper without a DOI was stored that way and hashed on its
author's initial.
The provider now returns \enquote{E. Fares}, hence another id, and
without care the next run of the same search would add every such paper
a second time, pending, next to the entry that already carries a
decision.
Nothing in the incoming paper points back at the old entry, so we work
from the other end: for each stored paper whose first author is surname
first, we compute the id it \emph{would} have in the right order, and
recognise the incoming paper by that.
The entry then takes the incoming authors (and with them the new id),
keeps its decision, and its note and decision records are migrated like
those of an adopted preprint.
This needs no knowledge of Scopus: a stored name that ends in initials
is surname first whoever supplied it, and the match is the same
title-and-surname identity used everywhere else.

<<review session functions>>=
def given_name_first_id(paper: Paper) -> str | None:
    """Return the id `paper` gets once its first author is reordered.

    None when nothing would change: the paper has a DOI (the hash is
    not its id), no authors, or a first author already in order.
    """
    if paper.doi or not paper.authors:
        return None
    reordered = given_name_first(paper.authors[0])
    if reordered == paper.authors[0]:
        return None
    return legacy_hash_paper_id(paper.title, [reordered])
@

\label{sec:review-renamed-authors}
DBLP gave the same trouble the other way round.  Its author names
carried a homonym number, \enquote{Wei Wang 0001}, until the provider
learnt to drop it (\cref{sec:utils-dblp-homonyms}), so a DBLP paper
without a DOI was stored hashed on [[0001]] and now arrives under
another id.  The cure is the same: [[homonym_free_id]] computes the id a
stored entry gets once the number is gone, and both kinds of alias go
into one lookup of \emph{renamed} entries, handled alike below.

The number can also arrive.  DBLP's search cache never expires
(\cref{sec:dblp-transient-failures}), so a rerun may be served the
numbered names recorded before the fix, and they would re-enter a
session that already holds the plain names as a second, pending entry.
We therefore drop the number from every incoming paper as well, before
its id is taken; whichever side still has it, the two then meet under
the plain name.  Doing so for every provider costs nothing, since only
DBLP writes names that end in a separate four-digit word.

<<review session functions>>=
def homonym_free_id(paper: Paper) -> str | None:
    """Return the id `paper` gets once DBLP's homonym number is dropped.

    None when nothing would change: the paper has a DOI (the hash is
    not its id), no authors, or a first author without the number.
    """
    if paper.doi or not paper.authors:
        return None
    stripped = strip_dblp_homonym_number(paper.authors[0])
    if stripped == paper.authors[0]:
        return None
    return legacy_hash_paper_id(paper.title, [stripped])


def without_homonym_numbers(paper: Paper) -> Paper:
    """Return `paper` with DBLP's homonym numbers dropped from its authors."""
    authors = [strip_dblp_homonym_number(a) for a in paper.authors or []]
    if authors == (paper.authors or []):
        return paper
    return replace(paper, authors=authors)
@

\label{sec:review-found-by}
Whichever way an incoming paper finds its decision (an entry already in
this run, a renamed or adopted entry, a previous decision, or a new one),
the decision records the search that returned it ([[record_search]]).  The
link has to be made here: this loop is the only place that still knows
which [[SearchResult]], and so which search record, a paper came from.
A link is a part's query, provider and [[filters_key]] of its filters,
exactly the key [[add_query_provider_pair]] recorded above, so the link
always names a stored record.
A decision rebuilt from the previous session copies that session's links,
and the new ones are added to them: a paper returned by three queries
keeps all three.

A merged result (the default [[search -n]] over several providers) is
recorded part by part (\cref{sec:search-result-parts} in the [[scholar]]
module): one record per provider, with that provider's own count and
error, never one record [[openalex, dblp]] whose failure would be
charged to every provider in it
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/164}).  Its papers are
still the deduplicated ones, so deduplication stays a matter of papers,
and each paper is linked to the parts that returned it
([[paper_search_links]]).  The decision's [[provider]] names the same
providers, so a paper only OpenAlex returned no longer reads as found
by DBLP as well.  Results built without parts (by [[rq]], by
[[--no-dedup]], in tests, by code outside scholar) are their own one
part; a [[Mock]] standing in for a result has no list of parts and is
treated so too.  Should a merged result's [[found_in]] not fit its
papers---code that filtered the papers after merging---the links are
computed again from the papers themselves.

<<review session functions>>=
def search_parts_of(result) -> list:
    """The single-provider searches of a `SearchResult`-like object."""
    parts = getattr(result, "parts", None)
    if isinstance(parts, list) and parts:
        return list(parts)
    return [result]


def paper_search_links(result) -> list[list[tuple[str, str, str]]]:
    """For each paper of `result`, the keys of the searches that returned it.

    A key is `(query, provider, filters key)` of one part of the result
    (see `search_parts_of`); a result that is not a merge links every
    paper to itself.
    """
    parts = search_parts_of(result)
    keys = [(p.query, p.provider, filters_key(p.filters)) for p in parts]
    if len(parts) == 1:
        return [[keys[0]] for _ in result.papers]
    found_in = getattr(result, "found_in", None)
    if not isinstance(found_in, list) or len(found_in) != len(result.papers):
        found_in = parts_returning(result.papers, parts)
    return [[keys[i] for i in indices] for indices in found_in]
@

<<convert search results to decisions>>=
decision_lookup: dict[str, ReviewDecision] = {
    get_paper_id(d.paper): d for d in session.decisions
}
renamed_lookup: dict[str, ReviewDecision] = {}
for d in session.decisions:
    for new_id in (given_name_first_id(d.paper), homonym_free_id(d.paper)):
        if new_id and new_id not in decision_lookup:
            renamed_lookup[new_id] = d
provenance_lookup: dict[str, ReviewDecision] = {}
for d in session.decisions:
    for entry in d.paper.published_versions or []:
        published_doi = entry.get("doi")
        if published_doi:
            key = f"doi:{Paper._normalize_doi(published_doi)}"
            provenance_lookup[key] = d

for result in results:
    for paper, links in zip(result.papers, paper_search_links(result)):
        found_by_provider = ", ".join(
            dict.fromkeys(provider for _, provider, _ in links)
        ) or result.provider
        paper = without_homonym_numbers(
            _normalize_paper_identifiers(paper)
        )
        paper_id = get_paper_id(paper)

        folded_to = resolve_folded_id(session.folded, paper_id)
        if folded_to != paper_id and folded_to in decision_lookup:
            target = decision_lookup[folded_to]
            if not plausibly_same_work(paper, target.paper):
                target.note_same_id_copy(paper, found_by_provider)
                continue
            target.paper = merge_version_of(target.paper, paper)
            target.provider = united_providers(
                target.provider, found_by_provider
            )
            for link in links:
                target.record_search(*link)
            continue

        existing = decision_lookup.get(paper_id)
        if existing is not None and is_same_id_namesake(paper, existing.paper):
            existing.note_same_id_copy(paper, found_by_provider)
            continue
        if existing is not None:
            try:
                existing.paper = existing.paper.merge_with(paper)
            except ValueError:
                pass
            existing.provider = united_providers(
                existing.provider, found_by_provider
            )
            for link in links:
                existing.record_search(*link)
            continue

        renamed = renamed_lookup.pop(paper_id, None)
        if renamed is not None:
            old_id = get_paper_id(renamed.paper)
            migrate_paper_id(old_id, paper_id)
            renamed.paper = replace(
                renamed.paper, authors=paper.authors
            ).merge_with(paper)
            renamed.provider = united_providers(
                renamed.provider, found_by_provider
            )
            decision_lookup.pop(old_id, None)
            decision_lookup[paper_id] = renamed
            for link in links:
                renamed.record_search(*link)
            continue

        adopted = provenance_lookup.get(paper_id)
        if adopted is not None:
            adopted.paper = merge_version_of(adopted.paper, paper)
            adopted.provider = united_providers(
                adopted.provider, found_by_provider
            )
            for link in links:
                adopted.record_search(*link)
            continue

        replaced = None
        for entry in paper.published_versions or []:
            published_doi = entry.get("doi")
            if not published_doi:
                continue
            old_id = f"doi:{Paper._normalize_doi(published_doi)}"
            replaced = decision_lookup.get(old_id)
            if replaced is not None:
                break
        if replaced is not None:
            migrate_paper_id(get_paper_id(replaced.paper), paper_id)
            replaced.paper = merge_version_of(paper, replaced.paper)
            decision_lookup[paper_id] = replaced
            provenance_lookup[old_id] = replaced
            for link in links:
                replaced.record_search(*link)
            continue

        # Check for previous decision (prefer session data over notes)
        prev_dec = prev_decision_lookup.get(paper_id)
        prev_record = None
        if not prev_dec and previous_decisions:
            prev_record = previous_decisions.decisions.get(paper_id)

        # Backward compatibility: if the paper gained a DOI, the previous
        # decision may have been keyed by the legacy hash id.
        if (prev_dec is None or prev_record is None) and getattr(paper, "doi", None):
            legacy_id = legacy_hash_paper_id(paper.title, paper.authors)
            if prev_dec is None:
                prev_dec = prev_decision_lookup.get(legacy_id)
                if prev_dec and not plausibly_same_work(paper, prev_dec.paper):
                    prev_dec = None
            if prev_record is None and previous_decisions:
                prev_record = previous_decisions.decisions.get(legacy_id)

        if prev_dec and is_same_id_namesake(paper, prev_dec.paper):
            prev_dec.note_same_id_copy(paper, found_by_provider)
            session.decisions.append(prev_dec)
            decision_lookup[paper_id] = prev_dec
            continue
        if prev_dec:
            decision = replace(
                prev_dec,
                paper=stored_paper_with(prev_dec.paper, paper),
                provider=united_providers(prev_dec.provider,
                                          found_by_provider),
                tags=list(prev_dec.tags),
                found_by=list(prev_dec.found_by),
                same_id_copies=list(prev_dec.same_id_copies),
            )
        elif prev_record:
            <<rebuild the decision from the old decision store>>
        else:
            # New paper, no previous decision
            decision = ReviewDecision(
                paper=paper,
                provider=found_by_provider,
                status=DecisionStatus.PENDING,
            )


        for link in links:
            decision.record_search(*link)
        session.decisions.append(decision)
        decision_lookup[paper_id] = decision
