\paragraph{A paper found again in the old decision store.}
\label{sec:decision-store-fallback}
When there is no session file, a paper's previous decision may be a
record of the old per-query store.  Until November 2026 its decision
was rebuilt around the copy the search returned alone, with only the
search's provider, which dropped the abstract, venue, URL and PDF link
that the record holds
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/194}): the same loss as
a session file's decision suffered before [[stored_paper_with]]
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/192}).  So the record's
paper is rebuilt as the restore does ([[paper_from_decision_record]])
and goes through the same rule as a session file's paper: the stored
values win, the returned copy fills the gaps, and a DOI-less record met
by a DOI copy found under its legacy hash id lets the DOI copy keep its
identity and version fields ([[merge_version_of]]).  The provider names
both the record's provider and the search's ([[united_providers]]).

The legacy-id lookup above takes a record without asking whether its
work is the one returned, unlike the session file's lookup, which
asks [[plausibly_same_work]].  A record of another work with the same
title and first author would then lend its abstract to the returned
paper, so the stored paper fills the returned one only when the two may
be one work; otherwise the returned copy stands alone, as before.  The
decision is still taken from the record, as it always was: which
decision a namesake should get is a question of matching, not of
keeping data, and is left as it is.

Unlike a session file's paper, a record's paper need not have the id it
is stored under.  The store keys a record by the id of the paper when
it was decided, but the rebuilt paper takes the DOI the record holds,
or one derived from an arXiv URL ([[_normalize_paper_identifiers]]),
so a record kept under a DOI-less hash id can rebuild with a DOI.
[[stored_paper_with]] handles two cases only: equal ids
([[merge_with]], which refuses papers with different ids) and a
DOI-less stored copy met by a DOI copy ([[merge_version_of]]).  A
stored copy with a DOI met by a returned copy without one is neither,
so then too the returned copy stands alone, as before.

<<review session functions>>=
def stored_paper_fills(returned: Paper, stored: Paper) -> bool:
    """Whether `stored_paper_with(stored, returned)` may fill `returned`.

    The two must be copies `stored_paper_with` can merge (equal ids,
    or a DOI-less stored copy and a returned copy with a DOI) and
    plausibly one work (`plausibly_same_work`).
    """
    mergeable = stored.id == returned.id or (
        not stored.doi and bool(returned.doi)
    )
    return mergeable and plausibly_same_work(returned, stored)
