The case is the one of \cref{sec:review-create-strict}'s fallback: a
query reviewed before session files existed, its decisions only in the
store, searched again by a provider that returns the paper without an
abstract.  The store is written with [[save_search_decisions]], which
is what remains of the code that wrote it, and nothing else is on disk.
The second test is a paper that gained a DOI since it was decided, so
its record is found under the legacy hash id; the third, a record of
another work with the same title and first author, twenty years older.
In both, the restore also brings the record back under its own id, a
DOI-less copy beside the DOI one, as an append leaves such copies for
[[sessions reconcile]] (\cref{sec:session-copies}); the tests look at
the DOI copy.

