\subsection{Copies of one work}
\label{sec:session-copies}

A session holds one decision per paper id, and two copies of one work
can still end up as two decisions, or two works as one (issue~\#170).
Search-time deduplication (\cref{sec:doi-less-copies} in the scholar
module) handles the copies within one result list; a session collects
many result lists, from many providers and many runs, so it has to ask
the same two questions itself.

\paragraph{Two works under one id.}
A paper without a DOI is identified by a hash of its title and its first
author's surname, and an editorial column or a talk given twice shares
that hash with a work of the same name years earlier.  Search-time
deduplication keeps two such records apart when their years are more
than one apart ([[plausibly_same_work]]); a session cannot.  Its
decisions, the notes, the per-query decision files and the paper
registry are all keyed by the id, so two works with one id would share
one decision whatever we did, and changing the id scheme to include the
year would orphan every stored decision and note of a DOI-less paper
(the rule \enquote{at most one year apart} is not even transitive, so no
year-based id could express it).  This is a known limitation: a
DOI-less identity cannot tell such namesakes apart.  What the session
can do is not pretend.  When a search returns a DOI-less paper whose id
a decision already holds but whose year is implausible for it, the
decision keeps its paper as it was---merging the two would give one
work the other's year, venue or abstract---and the newcomer is noted in
[[same_id_copies]] with its year, venue and provider.  The search link
is not recorded either: that search did not return this work.  The note
is persisted, so [[sessions show]] and the report can name both years
and let the user tell at a glance that two works probably share an id.
The same guard keeps a DOI paper from taking over the decision of a
DOI-less paper years away from it, which the backward-compatible lookup
by the legacy hash id would otherwise do.

<<review session functions>>=
def is_same_id_namesake(paper: Paper, held: Paper) -> bool:
    """Whether DOI-less `paper` shares `held`'s id but not its work.

    Both have the hash id of the same title and first author, and
    `plausibly_same_work` rules out that they are one work (their years
    are more than one apart).
    """
    return (
        not paper.doi
        and get_paper_id(paper) == get_paper_id(held)
        and not plausibly_same_work(paper, held)
    )
<<review imports>>=
from scholar.scholar import doi_copy_index, doi_copy_of, foldable_copy
from scholar.notes import NotesFileError, list_papers_with_notes
