The command's side: lock, load fresh, fold, save, then move the notes.
A dry run makes the same folds in memory and writes nothing, so it
lists exactly what a real run would fold, the pairs skipped for their
notes included.  A save that fails raises before any note has moved,
so the notes still match the file, which still holds both copies.

The notes are moved from the tombstones, not only from this run's
folds ([[move_stranded_notes]]): every folded-away id that still has a
note, whose new id has none, gets it moved.  So a reconcile can be run
again to repair what its notes step left undone: a note write that
failed after the session was saved, or a note that a process which had
loaded the session before the reconcile saved under the old id
afterwards.  When both ids have a note the old one stays, as
[[migrate_paper_id]] would otherwise drop it, and it is still found:
[[has_note]] looks a DOI paper's note up under its legacy hash id too.
A note whose old id the session still holds stays where it is: that
copy was kept apart (below), and its note belongs to it.

<<review session functions>>=
def reconcile_session(
    name: str, *, dry_run: bool = False
) -> list[tuple[str, str]]:
    """Fold the foldable copies of the saved session `name`.

    Runs under the session file's lock: loads the file, folds, saves,
    and then moves the notes of the folded copies to their new ids.
    With `dry_run`, lists the folds without writing anything. Returns
    the `(old id, new id)` of each fold (to be) made.
    """
    session = load_session(name, strict=True)
    if session is None:
        raise FileNotFoundError(f"Session not found: {name}")
    if dry_run:
        return reconcile_copies(session)
    with file_lock(session_file_path(session)):
        session = load_session(name, strict=True)
        folds = reconcile_copies(session)
        if folds:
            save_session(session)
    move_stranded_notes(session)
    return folds


def move_stranded_notes(session: ReviewSession) -> list[tuple[str, str]]:
    """Move notes left under folded-away ids to their new id.

    Only when the new id has no note and the session no longer holds a
    decision under the old id. Returns the moves made.
    """
    if not session.folded:
        return []
    noted = {note.paper_id for note in list_papers_with_notes()}
    held = {get_paper_id(d.paper) for d in session.decisions}
    moves = []
    for old_id in session.folded:
        new_id = resolve_folded_id(session.folded, old_id)
        if old_id in noted and new_id not in noted and old_id not in held:
            migrate_paper_id(old_id, new_id)
            noted.discard(old_id)
            noted.add(new_id)
            moves.append((old_id, new_id))
    return moves
@

\paragraph{Tombstones.}
A process that loaded the session before the reconcile still holds the
folded copy, and its next save would write it back: the merge adds a
paper only the file has, and keeps a paper only this process has.  So
the session file records every fold in [[folded]], a map from the
folded-away id to the id it was folded into, and the merge with the
file (\cref{sec:session-integrity}) applies it.  The map is united with
the file's on every save, like the search records, so a stale process
cannot drop it either.  [[apply_tombstones]] then looks at each
decision this process holds under a folded-away id.  The principle is
strict: \emph{folding happens only in [[sessions reconcile]]; a save
never folds a decision.}  Every attempt to let a save settle a stale
copy against its target by comparing decisions lost a decision to some
order of saves: with the tombstones applied before the merge, a stale
copy's keep overwrote a discard another process had saved on the
target; applied after it, the keep was copied onto the still
unscreened target, and a discard saved after that won.  So a save only
drops what this process did not touch:
\begin{description}
\item[Untouched] The copy is unscreened, its state is what this
  process loaded (or, for a copy this process created, it is
  unscreened all the same), it has no note, and the file no longer
  holds it: the reconcile removed it there.  Nobody has screened it, so
  it holds no decision to lose.  It is dropped, and its search links,
  other-work notes, provider and metadata go to the target, the
  metadata filling gaps only ([[merge_version_of]]) as in a fold, so an
  abstract a stale process added to its copy is not lost.
\item[Touched] This process decided it, tagged it, had an LLM classify
  it, or gave it a note; or the copy was decided already when this
  process loaded it; or the file still holds it, because it was kept
  apart before.  The copy stays apart, beside its target, whatever the
  target says.  A decided copy is never dropped by a save, even an
  unchanged one equal to its target: that would be a fold.  [[sessions show]] lists the pair as
  foldable or conflicting, and the next [[sessions reconcile]] settles
  it from a fresh load under the lock.
\end{description}
It runs after the three-way merge of the decisions and the disk
additions, so the target is the decision under the new id in the
merged session; a target only the file holds is in it only if the merge
added it, which it does not when this process loaded that id and then
gave it another (a preprint adoption renames a paper), so no rename is
undone by a duplicate.  Without a target, the copy stays and is listed
again.
An append ([[create_review_session]]) routes a search result that
arrives under a folded-away id to its target directly, recording the
search there, so a re-run of an old search does not bring the copy back
even in memory.  The folded-away id is a DOI-less hash, so it is shared
by namesakes (\cref{sec:session-copies}); a result whose year is
implausible for the target is a namesake of the folded copy, not the
copy, and is noted in the target's [[same_id_copies]] instead, as a
namesake of a held paper is.  Paper ids stored elsewhere in the session---the source
papers of a snowballing round, the drawn papers of an LLM review
sample---are repointed to the new id ([[repoint_folded_ids]]), so that
they still name a decision of the session.  A chain of folds (an id
folded into one that was later folded again) is followed to its end,
with a guard against a cycle that a hand-edited file could contain.

<<review session functions>>=
def resolve_folded_id(folded: dict[str, str], paper_id: str) -> str:
    """Follow the tombstones in `folded` from `paper_id` to its end."""
    seen = {paper_id}
    while paper_id in folded and folded[paper_id] not in seen:
        paper_id = folded[paper_id]
        seen.add(paper_id)
    return paper_id


def repoint_folded_ids(session: ReviewSession) -> None:
    """Repoint stored paper ids of folded-away copies to their target."""
    if not session.folded:
        return
    for snowball_round in session.snowball_rounds:
        snowball_round.source_papers = [
            resolve_folded_id(session.folded, paper_id)
            for paper_id in snowball_round.source_papers
        ]
    for sample in session.llm_samples:
        for paper in sample.papers:
            if paper.get("paper_id"):
                paper["paper_id"] = resolve_folded_id(
                    session.folded, paper["paper_id"]
                )


def apply_tombstones(
    session: ReviewSession,
    base_states: dict[str, tuple],
    disk_decisions: dict[str, ReviewDecision],
) -> None:
    """Drop the untouched copies `session` holds under folded-away ids.

    A copy is untouched when it is unscreened, unchanged since it was
    loaded, has no note and is no longer in the file; its links and
    metadata go to the target. Every other copy stays. Never changes or
    drops a decision.
    """
    if not session.folded:
        return
    by_id = {get_paper_id(d.paper): d for d in session.decisions}
    folded_held = [
        paper_id for paper_id in by_id
        if resolve_folded_id(session.folded, paper_id) != paper_id
    ]
    if not folded_held:
        return
    try:
        noted = {note.paper_id for note in list_papers_with_notes()}
    except NotesFileError:
        return
    dropped: set[int] = set()
    for paper_id in folded_held:
        decision = by_id[paper_id]
        target = by_id.get(resolve_folded_id(session.folded, paper_id))
        untouched = (
            is_unscreened(decision)
            and base_states.get(paper_id, decision_state(decision))
            == decision_state(decision)
            and paper_id not in noted
            and paper_id not in disk_decisions
        )
        if target is None or not untouched:
            continue
        <<unite the links of [[decision]] into [[target]]>>
        dropped.add(id(decision))
    if dropped:
        session.decisions = [
            d for d in session.decisions if id(d) not in dropped
        ]
        session._invalidate_cache()
