\subsection{Session Persistence}
\label{sec:session-persistence}

Sessions can be saved and loaded for interrupted workflows. The session file
includes [[search_records]] to track which query, filters, and limit were used
with which provider, and what each search returned (hits, error, retries;
absent in older files, which load as unknown hits and no error). Loading walks a backward-compatibility ladder: newest files
carry [[search_records]]; files from before filters were persisted carry only a
[[query_provider_pairs]] list, which we load with empty filters; and the oldest
files carry neither, so we reconstruct one record per provider by pairing the
single [[query]] with each provider in [[providers]].
Each decision carries its [[found_by]] links as a list of
[[{"query", "provider", "filters"}]] objects, [[filters]] holding the
record's [[filters_key]]; files from before the links existed
have none, and their decisions load with an empty list.

Files written before filters were part of a search's key hold links
without [[filters]].  Such a link is tied to a record when it is loaded,
if the session holds exactly one record with its query and provider:
then it can only mean that record.  Doing it at load time, rather than
whenever the link is read, matters for sessions that grow: a later run
of the same query with other filters adds a second record, and the old
link, still unresolved, would become ambiguous although it was certain
when the file was written.  Once resolved, it is saved with its filters
like any new link.  A link that matches two or more records stays
unresolved ([[None]]) and is written back without [[filters]], so the
file still says that we do not know.  One limit remains: a session that
ran one query with two filter sets \emph{before} this change kept only
the later run's record, and its links resolve to that record.  The
earlier run's filters were lost when the file was written, and no reader
can recover them.

Saving is where a session can lose its decisions, so [[save_session]] does
more than serialise: it merges what other processes saved since this one
loaded the session, warns when the save would still drop decisions, keeps
the previous file as [[<name>.json.bak]] and replaces the file
atomically.  \Cref{sec:session-integrity}, after the two functions,
explains why each safeguard is needed.

<<review session functions>>=
def _get_sessions_dir() -> Path:
    """Get the directory for storing review sessions."""
    path = get_data_dir() / SESSIONS_DIR
    path.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    return path
@

Papers store references and citations as lists of [[Paper]] objects, but JSON
cannot directly serialize these nested objects.
The [[Paper]] class provides [[to_dict()]] and [[from_dict()]] methods for
serialization: [[to_dict(include_refs_cites=True)]] serializes the paper with
its refs/cites (one level deep), and [[from_dict()]] reconstructs the paper
including any nested refs/cites.
This ensures enriched references and citations persist across session restarts,
avoiding expensive re-fetching from APIs.

A new session gets a file named after the session, or after the hash
of its query when it has no name ([[own_session_file_name]]).  A session
read from a file is saved back to that file, whatever its stored name
says, because a file renamed or copied by hand holds the name of
another session (\cref{sec:session-own-file}).

<<review session functions>>=
def own_session_file_name(name: str | None, query: str | None) -> str:
    """Return the file name a session's stored name (or query) maps to."""
    if name:
        return f"{name}.json"
    return f"{get_query_hash(query or '')}.json"


def session_file_path(session: ReviewSession) -> Path:
    """Return the path of the file `save_session` writes `session` to.

    That is the file the session was loaded from, if it was loaded;
    otherwise the file its name (or query) maps to.
    """
    if session._loaded_from is not None:
        return session._loaded_from
    return _get_sessions_dir() / own_session_file_name(
        session.name, session.query
    )
@

A command that is about to change many papers over a long time can keep
the file as it is now, so that the whole run can be undone.
[[.bak]] cannot do that: it is overwritten by every save, so after a run
that saves repeatedly it holds the state before the last save.
We copy the file's text under [[label]]: the copy is
[[<name>.json.<label>-<UTC time>.bak]], with the time as
[[YYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ]], written atomically.
Each run makes a new copy and does not replace the last one, because
the usual reason for a second run is that the first was interrupted:
the second run would otherwise overwrite the only copy of the original
with a half-enriched session.
We keep the newest [[RUN_COPIES_KEPT]] copies of a session and delete
the older ones after the new copy is written, so that the files do not
pile up (a session is megabytes) while an interrupted run that is
continued once or twice still leaves the original.
The time in the name sorts like the time itself, so the oldest copy is
the first in name order.
We copy the bytes on disk, not a serialisation of [[session]]: the file
is what the user would restore, and it may hold more than this process
loaded.
The copy covers the session file only.
Preprint adoption also rewrites the notes and the per-query decision
stores ([[migrate_paper_id]]), so after restoring the session from a
copy those stay keyed to the adopted ids.
The copy is made under the session's lock, so that no save is half done
when we read.
Its name does not end in [[.json]], which is what [[list_sessions]],
[[sessions_mentioning]] and the other code that reads every session file
look for, so the copy is not mistaken for a session (a test in the
command that uses it checks this).
The function returns [[None]] when there is no file yet, so that the
first save of a new session makes no copy of nothing.
[[_utcnow]] is the seam that lets a test choose the time.

<<review constants>>=
RUN_COPIES_KEPT = 3  # copies from before a run, per session and label
<<review session functions>>=
def _utcnow() -> datetime:
    return datetime.now(timezone.utc)


def keep_copy_before_run(
    session: ReviewSession, label: str, keep: int | None = None
) -> Path | None:
    """Copy the saved session file to `<name>.json.<label>-<time>.bak`.

    Returns the path of the copy, or `None` when the session has not
    been saved yet. Only the newest `keep` (default `RUN_COPIES_KEPT`)
    copies with this label are kept.
    """
    keep = RUN_COPIES_KEPT if keep is None else keep
    path = session_file_path(session)
    with file_lock(path):
        if not path.exists():
            return None
        stamp = _utcnow().strftime("%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ")
        copy = path.with_name(f"{path.name}.{label}-{stamp}.bak")
        atomic_write_text(copy, path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
        older = sorted(path.parent.glob(f"{glob.escape(path.name)}.{label}-*.bak"))
        for stale in older[:-keep] if keep > 0 else older:
            stale.unlink(missing_ok=True)
    return copy
@

<<review session functions>>=
def save_session(session: ReviewSession) -> Path:
    """
    Save a review session to disk.

    Decisions and research context that another process saved since
    `session` was loaded are merged in first (the changes made through
    `session` win). The previous file is kept as `<name>.json.bak`, and
    the file is replaced atomically.

    Args:
        session: The ReviewSession to save.

    Returns:
        Path to the saved session file.
    """
    session_path = session_file_path(session)
    logger.info("Saving session to %s", session_path)

    with file_lock(session_path):
        <<merge changes other processes saved>>
        <<write the session file>>
    session._baseline = SessionBaseline.of(session)

    logger.debug("Saved %d decisions (%d kept, %d discarded, %d pending)",
                len(session.decisions), len(session.kept_papers()),
                len(session.discarded_papers()), len(session.pending_papers()))
    return session_path


def load_session(
    name_or_query: str, *, strict: bool = False
) -> ReviewSession | None:
    """
    Load a review session from disk.

    Args:
        name_or_query: Session name or query string.
        strict: Raise `SessionFileError` when the session file exists
            but cannot be read, instead of logging a warning and
            returning None.

    Returns:
        The loaded ReviewSession, or None if not found (or, unless
        `strict`, unreadable).
    """
    logger.debug("Loading session: %s", name_or_query)
    sessions_dir = _get_sessions_dir()

    # Try exact name first
    session_path = sessions_dir / f"{name_or_query}.json"
    if not session_path.exists():
        # Try query hash
        session_path = sessions_dir / f"{get_query_hash(name_or_query)}.json"

    if not session_path.exists():
        logger.debug("Session not found: %s", name_or_query)
        return None

    logger.debug("Found session file: %s", session_path)
    try:
        return read_session_file(session_path)
    except SessionFileError as error:
        if strict:
            raise
        logger.warning("%s", error)
        return None


def read_session_file(
    session_path: Path, *, use_registry: bool = True
) -> ReviewSession:
    """
    Read one session file, recording its state as the session's baseline.

    With `use_registry=False` the papers are the file's own, never the
    `Paper` objects this process already holds for the same ids.

    Raises:
        SessionFileError: The file cannot be read or parsed.
    """
    try:
        with open(session_path, "r", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            data = json.load(f)
        
        session = ReviewSession(
            query=data["query"],
            providers=data["providers"],
            timestamp=datetime.fromisoformat(data["timestamp"]),
            name=data.get("name"),
            research_context=data.get("research_context"),
        )
        
        # Load search records with backward compatibility. Newest files store
        # full records; files from before filter persistence store only
        # (query, provider) pairs; the oldest store neither, so we rebuild
        # from the session query and provider list.
        if "search_records" in data:
            session.search_records = [
                SearchRecord(
                    query=rec["query"],
                    provider=rec["provider"],
                    filters=rec.get("filters"),
                    limit=rec.get("limit"),
                    hits=rec.get("hits"),
                    error=rec.get("error"),
                    retries=rec.get("retries") or 0,
                    total=rec.get("total"),
                    total_estimated=rec.get("total_estimated") is True,
                    searched_at=rec.get("searched_at"),
                    fetched_at=rec.get("fetched_at"),
                    from_cache=rec.get("from_cache"),
                    via=rec.get("via"),
                )
                for rec in data["search_records"]
            ]
        elif "query_provider_pairs" in data:
            for pair in data["query_provider_pairs"]:
                session.add_query_provider_pair(pair[0], pair[1])
        else:
            # Reconstruct from query and providers for old sessions
            for provider in data["providers"]:
                session.add_query_provider_pair(data["query"], provider)

        # Load snowball_rounds with backward compatibility
        if "snowball_rounds" in data:
            for round_data in data["snowball_rounds"]:
                session.snowball_rounds.append(SnowballRound(
                    timestamp=datetime.fromisoformat(round_data["timestamp"]),
                    source_papers=round_data["source_papers"],
                    direction=round_data["direction"],
                    papers_added=round_data["papers_added"],
                ))
        <<load the LLM review samples>>
        session.folded = dict(data.get("folded") or {})

        for dec_data in data["decisions"]:
            paper_data = dec_data["paper"]
            # Handle legacy format where "source" was a comma-separated string
            if "sources" not in paper_data and "source" in paper_data:
                paper_data["sources"] = [
                    s.strip() for s in paper_data["source"].split(",")
                ] if paper_data["source"] else []
            paper = Paper.from_dict(paper_data, use_registry=use_registry)
            paper = _normalize_paper_identifiers(paper)
            status = DecisionStatus(dec_data["status"])
            tags = dec_data.get("tags", []) or []

            # Load LLM-related fields with backward compatibility
            source_str = dec_data.get("source", "human") or "human"
            source = ReviewSource(str(source_str))
            is_example = dec_data.get("is_example", False) or False
            llm_confidence = dec_data.get("llm_confidence")
            found_by = without_superseded_links([
                _load_found_by_link(session, link)
                for link in dec_data.get("found_by") or []
            ])

            session.decisions.append(
                ReviewDecision(
                    paper=paper,
                    provider=dec_data["provider"],
                    status=status,
                    tags=tags,
                    source=source,
                    is_example=is_example,
                    llm_confidence=llm_confidence,
                    **{name: dec_data.get(name) for name in LLM_AUDIT_FIELDS},
                    found_by=found_by,
                    same_id_copies=list(dec_data.get("same_id_copies") or []),
                )
            )
        
        logger.info("Loaded session with %d papers", len(session.decisions))
    except (json.JSONDecodeError, KeyError, OSError, ValueError,
            TypeError) as e:
        raise SessionFileError(session_path, e) from e
    warn_about_unknown_sources(session_path, session)
    warn_about_foreign_name(session_path, session)
    session._baseline = SessionBaseline.of(session)
    session._loaded_from = session_path
    return session
@

A decision of unknown source (\cref{sec:review-source}) loads as it is,
and is treated as unreviewed; the user is told once per session file
and process, since [[save_session]] re-reads the file on every save and
a warning per save would bury the first.

<<review session functions>>=
_WARNED_UNKNOWN_SOURCES: set[tuple[str, tuple[str, ...]]] = set()


def warn_about_unknown_sources(session_path: Path, session) -> None:
    """Warn once that `session` holds decisions of unknown source."""
    values = tuple(sorted({
        d.source.value for d in session.decisions if d.source.is_unknown
    }))
    key = (str(session_path), values)
    if not values or key in _WARNED_UNKNOWN_SOURCES:
        return
    _WARNED_UNKNOWN_SOURCES.add(key)
    count = sum(1 for d in session.decisions if d.source.is_unknown)
    logger.warning(
        "%s: %d decision(s) with unknown source %s; they are kept as "
        "they are and treated as unreviewed (never cited) until a human "
        "decides them",
        session_path.name, count, ", ".join(repr(v) for v in values),
    )
@

The file is re-read, merged and replaced under [[file_lock]]
(\cref{sec:file-lock}), so that two processes saving one session at
the same moment take turns: the second re-reads only after the first
has written, and merges against what the first wrote.  The lock covers
only those milliseconds, never the time between loading a session and
saving it, which may be hours of enrichment or a whole TUI session; the
merge is what reconciles that.  The write itself serialises the merged
session:

<<write the session file>>=
# Convert to serializable format
data = {
    "query": session.query,
    "name": session.name,
    "providers": session.providers,
    "timestamp": session.timestamp.isoformat(),
    "research_context": session.research_context,
    "search_records": [
        {
            "query": r.query,
            "provider": r.provider,
            "filters": r.filters,
            "limit": r.limit,
            "hits": r.hits,
            "error": r.error,
            "retries": r.retries,
            "total": r.total,
            "total_estimated": r.total_estimated,
            "searched_at": r.searched_at,
            "fetched_at": r.fetched_at,
            "from_cache": r.from_cache,
            "via": r.via,
        }
        for r in session.search_records
    ],
    "snowball_rounds": [
        {
            "timestamp": r.timestamp.isoformat(),
            "source_papers": r.source_papers,
            "direction": r.direction,
            "papers_added": r.papers_added,
        }
        for r in session.snowball_rounds
    ],
    <<serialise the LLM review samples>>
    **({"folded": session.folded} if session.folded else {}),
    "decisions": [
        {
            "paper_id": get_paper_id(d.paper),
            "provider": d.provider,
            "status": d.status.value,
            "tags": d.tags,
            "source": d.source.value,
            "is_example": d.is_example,
            "llm_confidence": d.llm_confidence,
            **llm_audit_of(d),
            "found_by": [
                _found_by_link_data(link) for link in d.found_by
            ],
            **(
                {"same_id_copies": d.same_id_copies}
                if d.same_id_copies else {}
