A link is written as an object rather than a list, so that the two
forms tell themselves apart by the presence of [[filters]], not by
length or position: a resolved link carries its filters key (the empty
string for an unfiltered search), an unresolved old link carries none.
Reading applies the rule above, through [[search_record]], which answers
a link without filters only when one record has its query and provider.

<<review session functions>>=
def _found_by_link_data(link: tuple[str, str, str | None]) -> dict:
    """Serialise one `found_by` link; an unresolved link has no filters."""
    query, provider, key = link
    data = {"query": query, "provider": provider}
    if key is not None:
        data["filters"] = key
    return data


def _load_found_by_link(
    session: ReviewSession, data: dict
) -> tuple[str, str, str | None]:
    """Read one `found_by` link, resolving an old one if it is certain."""
    query, provider = data["query"], data["provider"]
    if "filters" in data:
        return (query, provider, data["filters"])
    record = session.search_record(query, provider)
    return (query, provider, record.key[2] if record else None)
@

The test makes the race happen on purpose.  Process~A's merge starts
process~B's save in a thread and gives it half a second.  Without the
lock, B writes its decision in that half second and A then replaces the
file with a merge of what it read before, so B's decision is gone.
With the lock, B waits, re-reads A's file and merges, and both
decisions survive.

<<test functions>>=
def test_simultaneous_saves_keep_both(tmp_path, monkeypatch):
    import threading
    import scholar.review as review

    monkeypatch.setenv("SCHOLAR_DATA_DIR", str(tmp_path))
    TestConcurrentSaves()._create()
    first = load_session("race")
    second = load_session("race")
    first.decisions[0].status = DecisionStatus.KEPT
    second.decisions[1].status = DecisionStatus.KEPT
    merge = review.merge_concurrent_changes
    threads = []

    def slow_merge(session, on_disk):
        if not threads:
            threads.append(threading.Thread(
                target=save_session, args=(second,)
            ))
            threads[0].start()
            threads[0].join(0.5)
        merge(session, on_disk)

    monkeypatch.setattr(review, "merge_concurrent_changes", slow_merge)
    save_session(first)
    threads[0].join(5)
    final = {d.paper.title: d.status for d in load_session("race").decisions}
    assert final["Paper 0"] == DecisionStatus.KEPT
    assert final["Paper 1"] == DecisionStatus.KEPT
@

\subsubsection{Session integrity}
\label{sec:session-integrity}

A session is the audit record of a review, so losing its decisions is the
worst failure this module can have.  It happened
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/107}): two sessions,
classified by an agent-driven workflow on 2026-09-03--06, were later found
with every decision back to pending and their research context gone,
while a third session from the same workflow was intact.  The files gave
the culprit away:
\begin{description}
\item[What survived] The session's query, creation timestamp and all
  82 (and 197) accumulated search records.  An append through
  [[create_review_session]] would have stamped a new timestamp and query,
  so neither an append nor a failed load followed by a rebuild did this.
\item[What was reset] Every decision had exactly the defaults of a
  freshly built [[ReviewDecision]] (pending, source human, no tags, no
  confidence), and the research context was [[None]], as on the day the
  sessions were created.  Paper identifiers were unchanged, so no id
  migration was involved.
\item[What was added] Most papers carried open-access status and a
  Crossref currency check (\cref{unpaywall-module,crossref-module}),
  which only enrichment writes, and one session had about two hundred
  more abstracts than its classification report counted.  The intact
  session carried no enrichment at all.
\end{description}
The decisions were therefore lost by the oldest bug in persistence, the
\emph{lost update}.  [[scholar enrich <session>]] loads a session, spends
a long time enriching every paper (with rate limits, hours for 1\,500
papers), and then saves the session object it loaded.  The workflow set
the research context and classified the papers while the enrichment ran,
and the enrichment's save replaced all of that with its stale copy from
before.  The TUI ([[run_review]]) and [[scholar verify]] follow the same
load-early, save-late pattern, and two agents appending to one session
can collide the same way.

We cannot make every command short, and locking a session for the hours
an enrichment runs would block the very classification the user wants to
do meanwhile.  Instead [[save_session]] merges: before writing, it reads
the file as it is on disk now and keeps whatever another process changed
there that this process did not.  To tell the two apart it needs a third
version, the \emph{baseline}: the decisions and research context as this
process loaded (or last saved) them.  For each field the rule is the one
of a three-way merge:
\begin{description}
\item[Changed here] This process's value differs from the baseline, so
  it is a deliberate change (a TUI decision, [[sessions decide]], an
  [[llm classify]] batch, a new research context) and is written.
\item[Changed only on disk] This process's value still equals the
  baseline but the file's does not, so another process changed it; the
  file's value is kept.  This is the case that the enrichment above got
  wrong.
\item[New on disk] A paper that is on disk but neither in this process's
  session nor in its baseline was added by another process (a concurrent
  [[search -n]]) and is kept.  A paper that \emph{is} in the baseline but
  no longer in this session was removed or re-keyed here (preprint
  adoption, \cref{preprint-module}; surname-first repair,
  \cref{sec:review-surname-first}) and stays removed.
\item[Changed on both sides] Both processes decided the same paper, and
  differently.  No rule can know which decision the user meant, so we
  keep this process's (the one being saved now, hence the more recent)
  and log a warning naming the session, the paper and both decisions.
  The other decision is still in the [[.bak]], so nothing is lost
  silently.
\end{description}
Paper metadata is merged field by field, ours first: for a paper both
hold, this process's value wins wherever it has one, and the file's
copy fills the fields this process's copy lacks
([[paper_filled_from]], below).  Until November 2026 this process's
copy won whole, so an enrichment another process saved between our
load and our save (abstracts, keywords, PDF links) was overwritten by a
save that had not touched the paper at all
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/194}).  What the
other process \emph{filled in} now survives; what it \emph{changed}
does not: a field both copies hold keeps ours, also when the other
process updated it after our load (see below).  Search records
and snowball rounds only ever accumulate, so we add any that only the
file has; a record is the
file's own when no record here has its key (query, provider and
filters, \cref{sec:search-record-key}), so a run of the same query with
other filters in another process is kept beside ours.  When both hold a
record of the same search, the two are not merged field by field but
one is kept, by the same rules as when a search is re-run
([[preferred_record]], \cref{sec:wider-record}): without that, a
process still holding the narrow record of a [[-l 5]] run saved it over
the [[-l 0]] record another process had saved meanwhile, and fifty
papers linked to a search that returns five.  This needs no baseline,
since the rules judge the records themselves; when they cannot tell the
two apart, ours is kept, as before.  The links need no repointing,
since both records have the same key.  So do a paper's
links to the searches that returned it ([[found_by]],
\cref{sec:review-found-by}): each is a fact about a search that ran, not
a judgement, so the links the file holds for a paper are added to the
links this process holds, whatever the decision rules decide.  The
same goes for the other works a search returned under a paper's id
([[same_id_copies]], \cref{sec:session-copies}).  After the
decisions are merged, the tombstones of folded copies are united with
the file's and applied ([[apply_tombstones]], \cref{sec:session-copies}),
so that a copy that [[sessions reconcile]] folded after this process
loaded the session is settled against the merged decision it was
folded into instead of being written back; the paper ids that
snowballing rounds and LLM review samples store are repointed at the
end.  The tombstones touch decisions only, the superseding of merged
records (\cref{sec:superseded-merged}) records and links only, and the
superseding runs after the tombstones over every decision held then,
so the links a dropped copy gave its target are repointed with all the
others, and no superseded record decides whether a copy is kept.

The baseline keeps one comparable state per paper id.  We compare the
whole decision state rather than the status alone, because a change of
tags or source (an LLM decision confirmed in the TUI) is as deliberate as
a change of status.  The classifier's record of its decision
(\cref{sec:llm-audit-fields}) belongs to that state too, so a decision
taken from the file brings its model, date and reason along instead of
pairing the file's status with this process's stale record.

<<review data structures>>=
def decision_state(decision: ReviewDecision) -> tuple:
    """Return the persisted decision fields of `decision`, comparably."""
    return (
        decision.status,
        tuple(decision.tags),
        decision.source,
        decision.is_example,
        decision.llm_confidence,
        *llm_audit_of(decision).values(),
    )


@dataclass
class SessionBaseline:
    """A session's decisions and research context as loaded or saved.

    `save_session` compares against it to tell the changes made in this
    process from the changes another process saved meanwhile.
    """
    decisions: dict[str, tuple]
    research_context: str | None
    record_keys: frozenset = frozenset()

    @classmethod
    def of(cls, session: "ReviewSession") -> "SessionBaseline":
        """Record the current state of `session`."""
        return cls(
            decisions={
                get_paper_id(d.paper): decision_state(d)
                for d in session.decisions
            },
            research_context=session.research_context,
            record_keys=frozenset(r.key for r in session.search_records),
        )
@

The baseline also keeps the keys of the session's search records.  The
merge needs them for one case only: to tell a legacy record that another
process replaced from one this process still holds
(\cref{sec:legacy-records}).

The merge itself applies the three rules.  A session constructed in
code has no baseline; then nothing of its own counts as unchanged, and
only papers new on disk are added.  A session that
[[create_review_session]] builds always has one, also when it loaded
nothing: the state it was built with, or the loaded file's baseline
when it continues that file (\cref{sec:session-own-file}).  The research context
gets one extra rule for this case: an empty context never replaces a
non-empty one, since there is no way to clear a context by accident that
we would want to honour.

<<review session functions>>=
def merge_concurrent_changes(
    session: ReviewSession, on_disk: ReviewSession
) -> None:
    """Merge into `session` what another process saved in `on_disk`.

    Decisions and research context that `session` still has as loaded
    (its baseline) take the on-disk value; papers and search records
    that only the file has are added; a paper both hold keeps the
    values of `session`, its empty fields filled from the file's copy.
    Changes made through `session` are kept.
    """
    base = session._baseline
    base_states = base.decisions if base else {}
    disk_decisions = {get_paper_id(d.paper): d for d in on_disk.decisions}
    <<find the legacy records this session replaced>>

    own_ids = set()
    for decision in session.decisions:
        paper_id = get_paper_id(decision.paper)
        own_ids.add(paper_id)
        theirs = disk_decisions.get(paper_id)
        if theirs is not None:
            decision.paper = paper_filled_from(decision.paper, theirs.paper)
            for link in theirs.found_by:
                decision.record_search(*replaced.get(link, link))
            for copy in theirs.same_id_copies:
                decision.add_same_id_copy(copy)
        if theirs is None or paper_id not in base_states:
            continue
        mine, base_state = decision_state(decision), base_states[paper_id]
        if decision_state(theirs) in (base_state, mine):
            continue
        if mine == base_state:
            decision.status = theirs.status
            decision.tags = list(theirs.tags)
            decision.source = theirs.source
            decision.is_example = theirs.is_example
            decision.llm_confidence = theirs.llm_confidence
            for name, value in llm_audit_of(theirs).items():
                setattr(decision, name, value)
        else:
            <<warn that both processes decided the paper>>

    for paper_id, theirs in disk_decisions.items():
        if paper_id not in own_ids and paper_id not in base_states:
            held = get_registry().register(theirs.paper)
            if plausibly_same_work(held, theirs.paper):
                theirs.paper = paper_filled_from(held, theirs.paper)
            session.decisions.append(theirs)
    session.folded = {**on_disk.folded, **session.folded}
    apply_tombstones(session, base_states, disk_decisions)

    <<merge research context from disk>>

    own_index = {r.key: i for i, r in enumerate(session.search_records)}
    links = linked_search_keys(session.decisions)
    provider_names = (
        decision_provider_names(session) | decision_provider_names(on_disk)
    )
    for record in on_disk.search_records:
        if record.key in own_index:
            i = own_index[record.key]
            session.search_records[i] = session_preferred_record(
                session.search_records[i], record, links, provider_names
            )
        elif record.key not in replaced:
            session.search_records.append(record)
    own_rounds = {r.timestamp for r in session.snowball_rounds}
    for snowball_round in on_disk.snowball_rounds:
        if snowball_round.timestamp not in own_rounds:
            session.snowball_rounds.append(snowball_round)
    <<merge the LLM review samples from disk>>
    supersede_merged_records(session)
    repoint_folded_ids(session)

    session._invalidate_cache()
@

A legacy record (\cref{sec:search-record-key}) is replaced by a re-run
of its query (\cref{sec:legacy-records}), and the replacement can
happen in either process.  Legacy records come only from old files and
are never created again, so the only way to lose one is to replace it,
and a save must not bring it back from the other side.
\begin{description}
\item[This process replaced it.]  The file still has the legacy record
  and we do not.  Adding it back, as the rule for records only the file
  has would, would undo the replacement on every save, and the file's
  links to it would come back with it.  So a file's legacy record is
  left out when this session has a record of the same query and
  provider, and the file's links to it are moved to that record, as the
  replacement moved ours.
\item[Another process replaced it.]  We still hold the legacy record,
  loaded before the other process saved its re-run, and the file no
  longer has it but has a record of the same query and provider.  If we
  kept ours, the file would get the legacy record back beside its
  replacement, with our papers still linked to it.  We can tell this
  apart from a legacy record the file never had because the record is
  in our baseline ([[SessionBaseline.record_keys]]): we loaded it from
  the file, and it has gone from there since.  So we drop it and move
  our links to the file's record of that query and provider, preferring
  one we did not load (the re-run), as the other process moved its own.
\end{description}
[[replaced]] maps each dropped legacy key to its successor; the file's
links pass through it as they are added, and ours are moved afterwards.

