\paragraph{Merging a paper both processes hold.}
The paper of a decision is not judged three ways like the decision.  A
paper's fields are facts gathered by searches and enrichment, and no
code in scholar clears one once it is known (in November 2026 the
sources assigned [[None]] to a paper field only in tests), so a field
that one copy has and the other lacks was gathered by one process and
not yet by the other, never removed on purpose.  Without a baseline
of the papers, the copies are merged by the rule a re-run already
applies to a stored paper ([[stored_paper_with]],
\cref{sec:review-create-strict}): [[Paper.merge_with]] with this
process's copy first.  Our value wins where both have one; the file's
fills the rest; sources and keywords are united and the higher
citation count kept.  The ids are equal by construction (the file's
decisions are looked up by our paper's id), which is all
[[merge_with]] asks.

That rule has a limit, and it is a real one.  Where both copies hold a
value, ours wins even when the other process \emph{replaced} it after
our load: a newer Crossref result replacing an older one
([[updated_by]], [[newer_versions]]; an empty list there is a
result, \enquote{checked, no notices}, not a gap) or a newer Unpaywall
result ([[oa_status]], [[oa_url]]) is undone by a save from a process
that loaded the paper with the older one.  Telling a value
the other process replaced from one we hold because we set it needs the
paper as loaded, a baseline per paper as the decisions have; that is
left for later.  Gaps, the common case of enrichment (abstracts,
keywords, PDF links), are filled.

An equal id is not always one work: two papers without a DOI share the
hash id of their title and first author (\cref{sec:session-copies}).
The file's copy may then be a namesake that another process put under
our id, and filling our paper from it would give our work its
abstract and venue.  So nothing is filled unless the copies may be one
work ([[plausibly_same_work]], as everywhere a title match decides).

[[merge_with]] builds a new [[Paper]] and changes neither copy, so the
object the paper registry holds for this id, which other parts of this
process may share, is not changed behind their back.  The decision
gets the new object only when the file's copy added something: a save
that has nothing to fill leaves the decision holding the very object it
held, as before.  A paper new on disk is the reverse case: the file's
copy is all we have of the decision, but this process may already hold
the paper (the registry has it), and then that copy is ours and the
file's fills its gaps.  Registering it also keeps the rule that a
loaded paper is the one this process shares.  If the copy we hold is
another work, the file's decision keeps the file's paper: the decision
was taken on that work, and our namesake must not stand in for it.

<<review session functions>>=
def paper_filled_from(ours: Paper, theirs: Paper) -> Paper:
    """`ours`, its empty fields filled from `theirs` (`Paper.merge_with`).

    Returns `ours` itself, unchanged, when `theirs` adds nothing or is
    another work (`plausibly_same_work`); otherwise a new `Paper`.
    Neither argument is modified.
    """
    if theirs is ours or not plausibly_same_work(ours, theirs):
        return ours
    merged = ours.merge_with(theirs)
    if all(
        getattr(merged, f.name) == getattr(ours, f.name)
        for f in fields(Paper)
    ):
        return ours
    return merged
