\paragraph{Saving to the file it was loaded from.}
\label{sec:session-own-file}
A session file holds the session's name, and the file has a name of its
own.  The two agree as long as only Scholar writes the files, but a
file copied or renamed by hand keeps the name inside: a
copy [[rapport-feedback-combined-run0.json]] still held
[[{"name": "rapport-feedback"}]]
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/181}).  [[save_session]]
used to derive the file from the stored name, so saving that copy wrote
into [[rapport-feedback.json]], another session's file.  The merge on
save then united the two sessions' papers and search records there,
and the file the user had opened never received the change.

Of the two names, the file's is the one the user chose: [[load_session]]
finds a session by its file name.  So [[read_session_file]] remembers
the path in [[_loaded_from]], and [[session_file_path]] returns it
instead of deriving one.  Everything that writes a session or sits next
to its file asks [[session_file_path]]: the lock, the re-read of the
merge, the [[.bak]] and the atomic write in [[save_session]], the copy
of [[keep_copy_before_run]] and the lock of [[reconcile_session]].
A session that was never read from a file still gets the file its name
maps to.

[[create_review_session]] builds a new session object around the loaded
one, and there the loaded file is not always the new session's.  The
append used to look its predecessor up by one key, the [[-n]] name or
else the query, and [[load_session]] tries that key as a file name
before the query's hash.  So [[search foo --review]] (no name) loaded
[[foo.json]] even when that file held the session named [[foo]] for
another query, and [[search foo -n foo]] loaded the unnamed session of
query [[foo]] from its hash file when there was no [[foo.json]].  In
both the new session is not the loaded one: it has another name, and
before \url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/181} it went to its
own file and left the loaded one alone.  Carrying the path over would
save it into the other session's file, the very mixing we set out to
stop.  The append now loads only its own file
(\cref{sec:append-own-file}), but that file can still hold another
session: a copy made by hand, such as the hash file of query [[q]]
holding a session of another query, or [[a.json]] holding [[b]].
The path is therefore carried over only when the new session is the
loaded one: its identity (name, or query when unnamed) maps to the same
file as the loaded session's.  We compare the file names that the
identities map to rather than the names themselves, because two unnamed
sessions have equal names ([[None]]) whatever their queries.
The other way the two can belong together needs no rule: when
[[-n a]] opens the renamed copy [[a.json]] holding [[b]], the new
session is named [[a]], and the file its name maps to is [[a.json]]
already.

<<review session functions>>=
def appends_to_loaded_file(
    previous: ReviewSession, name: str | None, query: str
) -> bool:
    """Tell whether a session `name` (or `query`) continues `previous`.

    True when the new session should be saved into the file `previous`
    was loaded from: both identities map to the same file name.
    """
    return previous._loaded_from is not None and (
        own_session_file_name(previous.name, previous.query)
        == own_session_file_name(name, query)
    )
@

We neither rename the file nor rewrite the stored name: either would
decide which of the two names is right, and only the user knows whether
the copy was deliberate.  But the user should know that the names
disagree, because the name the session shows (in [[sessions list]], in
reports) opens a different file, or none.  The warning names the file,
the stored name and the file that name maps to.  It comes once per file
and process, since [[save_session]] re-reads the file on every save.

<<review session functions>>=
_WARNED_FOREIGN_NAMES: set[tuple[str, str]] = set()


def warn_about_foreign_name(session_path: Path, session) -> None:
    """Warn once that the file holds a session whose name maps elsewhere."""
    own = own_session_file_name(session.name, session.query)
    key = (str(session_path), own)
    if session_path.name == own or key in _WARNED_FOREIGN_NAMES:
        return
    _WARNED_FOREIGN_NAMES.add(key)
    logger.warning(
        "%s holds session %r, whose name maps to %s; it is saved back to "
        "%s, not to %s (open it as %r)",
        session_path.name, session.name or session.query, own,
        session_path.name, own, session_path.stem,
    )
