\paragraph{An append loads only its own file.}
\label{sec:append-own-file}
Not carrying the path kept the other session's \emph{file} intact, but
not its decisions.  The append was still built from the session it had
loaded: [[search foo --review]] took over every decision of the named
session [[foo]], whatever its query, and saved them into the hash file
of query [[foo]], which then listed papers query [[foo]] never returned,
kept or discarded by another review, and nothing said so
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/213}).  [[search foo -n
foo]] did the same with the unnamed session's decisions when [[foo.json]]
did not exist yet.

The cause is that [[load_session]] answers another question than the
append asks.  It finds the session a \emph{user} means by a name or a
query, as [[sessions show X]] does, so it tries [[X]] both ways.  An
append needs the session it continues, which is the one it will be saved
into: the file [[own_session_file_name]] gives for the new session's
name, or for its query when it has no name.  [[load_own_session]] loads
that file and no other.  It finds every session Scholar wrote under
that identity, since Scholar has named the files this way since session
files exist: [[save_session]] wrote [[<name>.json]] or the query's hash
from the first version of this module on.  What it no longer finds is a
file that only a hand rename made, such as an unnamed session of query
[[q]] moved to [[q.json]]; that file is now the session named [[q]], and
[[-n q]] continues it.

The other candidate is not ignored in silence.  A user who ran
[[search foo --review]] against the named session [[foo]] until now
would otherwise see an empty review and believe the decisions lost, so
when the file the old lookup would have tried exists, one line names it
and says how to continue it ([[-n foo]] for a named session, the search
without [[-n]] for an unnamed one).  The level depends on what the
append does.  When its own file does not exist, the append starts a new
session where the old lookup would have continued the other one, so the
user most likely meant that one: a warning, which the command prints.
When its own file exists, the append continues its own session, as the
user asked, and the other file is only a pointer, logged at INFO
([[--verbose]] shows it), so that a session's every append does not
repeat it.

The hint looks for [[<query>.json]], and a query is not always a valid
file name: one longer than the file system allows makes [[exists]] raise
[[OSError]] (\enquote{File name too long}), which made [[load_session]]
fail the whole unnamed search.  Such a file cannot exist, so the error
reads as absent.

