\paragraph{The baseline belongs to the file.}
The merge of a save (\cref{sec:session-integrity}) judges each decision
against the session's baseline: a decision that is still as it was
loaded is not this process's, so the file's value wins.  A baseline
therefore describes one file, the one it was taken from.
[[create_review_session]] used to hand the loaded session's baseline to
the new session whether or not the new session continues that file
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/207}).  In the appends
above that saved into another file than the one they loaded (before
\cref{sec:append-own-file}), the merge then judged the other file
against the loaded file's baseline.  Say [[search foo --review]] loaded
[[foo.json]] (session [[foo]] of query [[bar]]) and saved into the hash
file of query
[[foo]], which holds decisions of its own.  Two things went wrong.
\begin{itemize}
\item A paper that the target file has decided and the search returned
  again, but that the loaded file does not hold, is a new, pending
  paper of the new session and is not in the loaded baseline.  Nothing
  said it was unchanged, so the pending decision replaced the file's.
\item A paper whose id the append changed (a DBLP homonym number
  dropped, \cref{sec:review-renamed-authors}) has its old id in the
  loaded baseline and no longer in the session.  The merge read that as
  \enquote{this process renamed it} and did not add the file's paper
  under the old id: the target file lost that decision altogether.
\end{itemize}
Starting without a baseline, as a session that loaded nothing does,
mends the second but not the first, and it does harm of its own: without
a baseline every decision the session holds counts as this process's
change, so for a paper both files hold the loaded file's decision
replaced the target's, and the loaded file's research context replaced
the target's too.  The loaded baseline did neither, by accident: it
records the loaded file's decision and context as unchanged, so the
merge took the target's.

What the merge needs to know is what this process changed after it
built the session.  So a session that will be saved into another file
takes as its baseline the state it holds when [[create_review_session]]
returns it.  What the user decides afterwards is not in that state and
wins as before; everything else counts as unchanged, so where the
target file differs (its own decision of a paper, its research context)
the file's value is kept, and the target's papers that the session does
not hold are added, under whatever ids they have.  The research context
follows the same rule: an existing target without a context keeps its
empty one, while a target that does not exist yet gets the loaded
file's context, since there is nothing to merge.

That the append carried the loaded file's other decisions into the
target at all came from the lookup that found the loaded file
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/213}), which this baseline
does not change, and it left one case unmended.  After the renamed id
the target held the work twice: its own decision under the old id and
the loaded file's under the new one, possibly opposite (discarded and
kept).  Nothing reports the pair: [[foldable_copies]],
[[conflicting_copies]], [[alike_copies]] and [[possible_copies]] all
pair a DOI-less copy with a DOI copy, and here both copies are DOI-less,
their ids differing in the surname that the homonym number made.  Only
the lookup fix could keep the loaded decision out of the target, and
it does: an append now loads only the file it is saved to
(\cref{sec:append-own-file}), so no append from
[[create_review_session]] saves into another file than it loaded.  The
built baseline still serves the sessions that loaded nothing (below)
and an own file that holds another session, a copy made by hand
(\cref{sec:session-own-file}): its identity maps elsewhere, so the
append does not count as continuing it and takes the built baseline,
although it saves back into the file it loaded.  There the second wrong
above remains: a paper whose id the append changed is not in the built
baseline under its old id, so the merge adds the file's copy back
beside the renamed one.  Only hand copies reach it.

A session that continues the file it loaded keeps the loaded baseline
instead.  It records what was loaded, so a decision another process
saved meanwhile still wins over a decision this process only loaded,
and a paper that this append renamed is not brought back from the file
under its old id.  A baseline taken after the build would know neither,
though the loaded one lacks the papers new in this run; the built state
fills that gap (below).  A session that loaded nothing takes its built
state too (below).

