\paragraph{Papers new in a same-file append.}
The loaded baseline has a gap of its own: it knows only the papers that
were loaded.  A paper that this run's search adds is pending in the
session and absent from the baseline, so the merge counted the pending
decision as this process's own.  When another process appended to the
same file, found the same paper and decided it before we saved, our
pending decision replaced its decision
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/214}).  That is the first
of the wrongs of the other-file case above, and the mend is the same:
a paper the loaded baseline lacks gets the state it was built with, so
an untouched pending decision yields to the file's.

Only the missing entries are added.  The loaded ones stay, so an id
that this append renamed away keeps its loaded entry, and the merge
still reads its absence from the session as a rename and does not bring
it back from the file.  The search-record keys and the research context
stay as loaded too: the keys tell the merge which legacy records it
loaded from the file (\cref{sec:legacy-records}), which a record of
this run never is, and the context is a single value that the loaded
baseline already holds.

