\paragraph{A session that loaded nothing.}
The same race has a third form, with no file at all to begin with.  Two
processes run [[search foo -n new]] at the same time: both find no
session, both build one from the same results, and the second decides a
paper and saves first, creating the file.  The first process then saves
a session that loaded nothing, so it had no baseline, and every decision
it holds counted as its own: its pending decision replaced the second
process's.  A brand-new session is no different from a session saved
into another file than it loaded: what it holds when
[[create_review_session]] returns it is what it built, not what the
user decided, so it takes that state as its baseline as well.  The same
goes for a session rebuilt from the old per-query decision store
(\cref{sec:decision-store-fallback}), which loaded no session file
either.

Nothing else depended on such a session having no baseline.  Its built
baseline holds only papers that the session holds, so the papers only
the file has are still added; its research context is empty unless the
caller sets one after the build, and an unchanged empty context then
takes the file's, as the rule for a missing baseline did, while a
context set after the build is a change and wins, as a non-empty one
did; it holds no legacy records, since every search records its limit
(\cref{sec:search-record-key}), so the legacy-record rule finds nothing
to drop; and [[warn_if_decisions_dropped]] reads the baseline only to
count the decisions this process reset to pending, which with a built
baseline are those decided at the build (restored from the decision
store) and pending at the save.  A session constructed in code, not
through [[create_review_session]], still has no baseline.

Let's verify it with real processes, as for the same-file append.

<<test functions>>=
