\paragraph{No record was loaded from the target.}
The built baseline holds the decisions as built, but not the keys of
the search records as built.  Those keys tell the merge which records
it loaded from the file it saves to, and it drops a legacy record of
ours that is in them and gone from the file, in favour of the file's
record of the same query and provider, because it reads that as
another process having replaced it (\cref{sec:legacy-records}).  A
session that does not continue a loaded file loaded no record from its
target: its records are this run's or the loaded file's, and the
target never had them.  With the built keys, a legacy record of this
run (a result that brings neither filters nor a limit, as a hand-built
result can) was dropped as soon as another process had saved a record
of its query and provider, under other filters, into the target; with
no baseline it was kept, beside the other.  (A record of the same key
is another matter: the two are one search, and the better-ranked one is
kept whatever the baseline.)  So the keys are empty for such a session.
A same-file append keeps the loaded keys, for the same reason.

<<empty the record keys of the built baseline>>=
session._baseline = replace(built, record_keys=frozenset())
