\subsection{Search parameters and research context}

Sessions built before query-provider pairs were recorded only have the
base query and the provider list, so the pairs are reconstructed from
those when missing.

<<review report generation>>=
def get_query_provider_pairs(session: ReviewSession) -> list[tuple[str, str]]:
    """Return query/provider pairs with backward-compatible fallback."""
    if session.query_provider_pairs:
        return session.query_provider_pairs
    return [(session.query, provider) for provider in session.providers]


def provider_queries_differ(session: ReviewSession) -> bool:
    """Whether any provider-specific query differs from the base query."""
    pairs = get_query_provider_pairs(session)
    return any(query != session.query for query, _ in pairs)
@

A report built on a session where a provider failed must not let the
reader assume that provider was searched.  [[search_outcomes]] pairs
each query and provider with the label of what the search returned
([[hits_label]], \cref{sec:search-result-error}): a count, or
\enquote{failed} with the reason.  Records from before outcomes were
stored---and sessions so old that they have no records at all---get an
empty label, since we do not know.  The provider table then appears
also when every provider ran the base query but one of them failed, and
it gains a hits column whenever at least one outcome is known; a report
built from an old session therefore looks exactly as it did.

A query can also be run twice on one provider with different filters,
say once over the full text and once with [[--in title-abstract]]
(\cref{sec:search-record-key}).  Those are two searches with two
counts, and a table of query and provider alone would show two rows
that look the same.  So each row carries the search's filter flags,
written as [[scholar search]] takes them ([[filter_flags]],
\cref{sec:filter-flags} in the [[scholar]] module), in a column that
appears when any search had filters; and the table appears whenever two
searches share their query and provider ([[search_table_needed]]),
since only the table tells them apart.

<<review report generation>>=
def search_outcomes(
    session: ReviewSession,
) -> list[tuple[str, str, str, str]]:
    """Return `(query, provider, flags, outcome label)` for each search.

    `flags` are the filter flags of the search, as `scholar search`
    takes them (empty when it had none).
    """
    if not session.search_records:
        return [(q, p, "", "") for q, p in get_query_provider_pairs(session)]
    return [
        (
            r.query, r.provider, " ".join(filter_flags(r.filters)),
            search_outcome_label(r),
        )
        for r in session.search_records
    ]


def search_dates(session: ReviewSession) -> list[str]:
    """The dates label of each search, parallel to `search_outcomes`."""
    if not session.search_records:
        return ["" for _ in get_query_provider_pairs(session)]
    return [search_dates_label(r) for r in session.search_records]


def searches_share_query(session: ReviewSession) -> bool:
    """Whether two recorded searches have the same query and provider."""
    pairs = [(r.query, r.provider) for r in session.search_records]
    return len(pairs) != len(set(pairs))


def search_table_needed(session: ReviewSession) -> bool:
    """Whether the report needs its table of provider searches."""
    return (
        provider_queries_differ(session)
        or any_search_failed(session)
        or searches_share_query(session)
        or len(searched_queries(session)) > 1
        or any(
            r.hits is not None or r.searched_at or r.fetched_at
            for r in session.search_records
        )
    )


def any_search_failed(session: ReviewSession) -> bool:
    """Whether any recorded search failed."""
    return any(r.error for r in session.search_records)
@

The parameters above the table used to be the session's [[query]] and
[[providers]], which each append overwrites: a session of six searches
on six providers reported \enquote{The search covered scopus}, the
provider of the last append
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/163}).  They are now
derived from all search records: every provider any record names (the
providers of a merged record each count, \cref{sec:merged-records}),
the one query if all records share it and otherwise their number, and
the period in which the databases answered ([[search_source_date]]),
with the number of searches whose date is unknown.  A provider is
listed among those searched only if it answered at least one search,
as in the caption of the audit table: one that was skipped or failed
outright every time was not searched, and the report says so, with the
reason, under \enquote{Not searched}, so that a reader sees the gap
instead of a database the review never reached.  A provider that an
old merged record with papers names as failed may have answered part
of that search or nothing of it, which the record cannot tell; it is
listed among the providers searched, with the note the caption gives
it, \enquote{failed or incomplete in a merged record}
([[searched_providers_text]]), so the report and the caption say the
same.  The table itself
appears whenever a record knows its outcome or its dates, so that each
search is described with them, in a Date column when any date is
known; only a session whose records know neither, from before outcomes
were kept, still gets the table under the old conditions alone, and
its report looks as it did.  Sessions without any records fall back to
the session's own fields.

\paragraph{Searches without a recorded outcome}
\label{sec:unrecorded-outcome}

A record written before outcomes were kept has neither hits nor an
error ([[outcome_unknown]]).  Every search since records its number of
hits, also zero, and a failure records its reason, so the pair
[[hits is None]] and no error marks exactly those old records.  Such a
record says that the provider was queried, not that it answered:
DBLP served only its anti-bot page in the autumn of 2026, and a session
searched then holds DBLP records that look exactly like those of a
provider that answered
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/188}).  Counting
them as answered, as the report and the audit caption did, named DBLP
among the databases searched.  Counting them as failed would be as
wrong the other way.

So a provider counts as searched when one of its records answered, or
when a paper of the session names it ([[decision_provider_names]]: the
decision's [[provider]], split and compared as the audit rows split it,
[[PROVIDER_SEPARATORS]], ignoring case).  A paper the provider returned
shows that one of its searches answered.  The name is not always that:
before one record was kept per provider search
(\cref{sec:merged-records}), every paper of a merged search was given
every merged provider's name, whichever returned it, so such a session
could name a provider that only served an error page.  The names are
therefore read through the evidence the decision holds
([[decision_provider_evidence]], \cref{sec:provider-evidence}), as the
audit rows read them.  A provider known only from
records without an outcome is listed apart, under \enquote{Outcome not
recorded} ([[unrecorded_providers]]), and not under \enquote{Not
searched}, which would claim it was never reached.  This also holds
when another of its searches failed: the old one may have answered.
The audit caption applies the same rule (\cref{sec:audit-databases}).

The rule describes what the report claims, not which record a session
keeps.  [[record_rank]] still ranks such a record as answered, so a
failed re-run does not replace it while a paper links to it or names
its provider: the old run may have answered with them, and replacing
it would turn that into a failure.  Otherwise the failed re-run
replaces it (\cref{sec:unknown-outcome-replaced}).  A failed record
never counts as searched, whatever the papers say, so the report then
lists the provider under \enquote{Not searched} with the failure as
the reason, unless another of its records answered, or has no outcome
either and is listed under \enquote{Outcome not recorded}.

