\paragraph{What a paper shows of its providers}
\label{sec:provider-evidence}

A decision's [[provider]] says which providers returned the paper,
except when an old merged search wrote it.  Until
\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/164} a merged search gave
every paper the merged string, \enquote{openalex, dblp}, whichever
provider returned it (\cref{sec:merged-records}).  Sessions searched
that way while DBLP served only its anti-bot page name DBLP on papers it
never returned: in the audit rows, through them in the caption's
\enquote{Databases:} sentence, and through [[decision_provider_names]]
in the report's searched providers
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/191}).  Nor can a
string with several names be taken as a merged search's and discarded:
since \#164 a paper returned by two per-provider searches names both,
and the repair of the sessions damaged by \#192 united the stored strings
with the providers of each decision's links.

So a name counts when the decision holds evidence for it.  Two things
are evidence.  One is a link to a single-provider search
([[found_by]], \cref{sec:review-found-by}): such a record was written
only for a provider whose search returned the paper.  A link to a merged
record proves nothing, for the same reason as its string.  The other is
the paper's [[sources]], which each provider sets to its own name on the
papers it converts and which the search merge unions over the copies
it deduplicates; a paper that only OpenAlex returned has the one source
[[openalex]].  Names are compared ignoring case and split
on [[PROVIDER_SEPARATORS]], as the rows split them: the string, each
source and each link's provider alike.  A source can itself be a joined
string, since the notes-module fallback of [[create_review_session]]
([[paper_from_decision_record]]) stores the old record's provider as
the paper's only source, and a link counts
as single-provider only when its provider splits into one name,
whatever the separator.

Four cases keep the string as it is.  A paper without sources, as in
sessions from before sources were stored, has nothing that can rule a
name out, and its names all count, as they did.  A single-provider link
confirms a name and adds one, but it does not rule any out: links are
younger than the merged strings (they are kept since
\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/128}), and a paper that
an old run returned and a later run returned again carries only the
later run's link, so its links do not show that the old run did not
return it (\cref{sec:unknown-outcome-replaced} makes the same point for
records).  Sources do not have that gap, as the next paragraph but one
explains.  Evidence that names none of the string's providers, such
as an enrichment source alone ([[s2]]) or the fallback's placeholder
[[previous]], contradicts the string rather than choosing among its
names, and is no better than none: dropping every name would leave the
row without a database at all.  A string with one name was never
written by a merged search, which always wrote two or more, so it
stands even when the sources disagree; the previous case already keeps
it, since evidence against its only name is evidence against every
name.  And a name that is not one of
the search providers in [[PROVIDER_FULL_NAMES]], such as [[snowball]],
which [[united_providers]] can join to a search's name, is not a
database a merged search could have named.

The two kinds of evidence are not equally clean, and they are not used
alike.  Sources also grow without a search: enrichment merges each
looked-up record into the paper ([[Paper.merge_with]] in
[[enrich_paper]]), which unions the enriching provider's name---S2,
arXiv, OpenAlex, IEEE, WoS or Scopus---into the sources, and preprint
adoption unions in its candidate's sources (\cref{preprint-module}),
which may name the provider that returned the preprint rather than the
published version.  So a source only confirms a name the string already
has; it never adds one, else every enriched paper would gain a database
that never searched it.  The error this leaves is in the old direction:
a provider that enriched a paper but did not return it is still named
if the merged string names it.  DBLP, the case that prompted this,
enriches nothing and is not a preprint candidate source (the title
search asks OpenAlex and arXiv, the other tiers copy the paper's own
sources), so a [[dblp]] in the sources means DBLP returned the paper.
Its absence tells as much: before \#164 a re-run gave the decision both
the paper as returned and the merged string of that run, so the sources
and the string come from the same search.  A single-provider link does
add its provider when the string lacks it, since the link itself is
proof; the order of the string is kept, and such names follow it.  On
the real sessions no decision with several names has a single-provider
link but no sources, so the links' part changes no row there.

One more limit comes from the paper registry: [[Paper.from_dict]]
returns the registered object for an id it already holds, so two
decisions of a session that share a paper id share one [[Paper]], and
the second is judged by the sources of the copy loaded first.

The rule was checked against copies of the real sessions before it was
adopted (70 sessions, November 2026).  A provider it drops from every
paper of a session is DBLP in sessions whose merged searches recorded
its failure or no outcome, OpenAlex where its merged search failed and
in two sessions (7\,892 papers) in which no paper has [[openalex]] in
its sources, and IEEE and S2 in three sessions where no paper has them
in its sources.  The absence is evidence because each provider names
itself on every paper it converts and the merge unions the sources of
every copy it combines, so a provider that returned any paper of the
session would appear in that paper's sources.  As a cross-check, none
of the 119\,726 names the rule drops there is the provider whose own
page the paper's [[url]] is.

