def decision_provider_evidence(decision: ReviewDecision) -> list[str]:
    """The provider names of `decision` that its evidence supports.

    When the paper has `sources`, a name of the `provider` string
    counts only if a source or a single-provider search link
    (`found_by`) names it. Without sources, when the evidence names
    none of the string's providers (so also for a string of one name),
    and for names not in `PROVIDER_FULL_NAMES`, the string stands.
    Single-provider links add their providers at the end.
    """
    def split(text: str | None) -> list[str]:
        return [
            part.strip() for part in PROVIDER_SEPARATORS.split(text or "")
            if part.strip()
        ]

    names: list[str] = []
    for part in split(decision.provider):
        if part.lower() not in {n.lower() for n in names}:
            names.append(part)
    linked = [
        parts[0] for _, provider, _ in decision.found_by
        if len(parts := split(provider)) == 1
    ]
    sources = [part for source in decision.paper.sources or []
               for part in split(source)]
    if sources:
        attested = {n.lower() for n in linked + sources}
        kept = [
            name for name in names
            if name.lower() in attested
            or name.lower() not in PROVIDER_FULL_NAMES
        ]
        if any(name.lower() in PROVIDER_FULL_NAMES for name in kept):
            names = kept
    for provider in linked:
        if provider.lower() not in {n.lower() for n in names}:
            names.append(provider)
    return names
@

Let's verify the rule: sources and single-provider links confirm a
name, a merged link does not, a link without sources rules nothing
out, nothing at all keeps every name, a lone
name stands, case and separators do not matter, a source cannot add a
name but a link can, and a name no search writes is kept.  Then the
splitting: a joined source confirms each of its names, evidence against
every name keeps the string, and a link joined by any separator is a
merged one.

