\subsection{Citation keys}
\label{sec:cite-keys}

The report and the \LaTeX{} synthesis both write a [[.bib]] file and
cite into it, and they used to build their keys in two different ways:
the report as [[surname{year}_{index}]], taking the last word of the
first author's name, so that \enquote{Smith, John} came out as
[[john2024_0]]; the synthesis as [[surname{year}]] with a letter suffix
from [[b]] on and [[n.d.]] for a missing year. Both now use the one
scheme in this section (issue~\#85), which also gives a reader one rule
to look a key up by.

A key is the first author's surname, lower-cased and reduced to letters
and digits, followed by the year, or [[nd]] when the year is unknown.
The surname is the part before the comma in \enquote{Smith, John} and
the last word otherwise, which is how both forms occur in provider
data. Reducing to letters and digits is what keeps a key safe for
biber, whose parser rejects apostrophes, whitespace, quotes, braces and
the BibTeX delimiters (an \enquote{O'Brien} once aborted a whole
bibliography); a letter outside ASCII, as in \enquote{Müller}, is kept,
since biber reads UTF-8 keys. A paper without authors, or with a
surname that has no letters or digits at all, is filed under
[[unknown]].

<<review report generation>>=
def author_surname(author: str) -> str:
    """Return the surname in `author` ("John Smith" or "Smith, John")."""
    author = author.strip()
    if "," in author:
        return author.split(",")[0].strip()
    parts = author.split()
    return parts[-1] if parts else author


def cite_key_base(paper: Paper) -> str:
    """Return the citation key of `paper` before any collision suffix."""
    surname = author_surname(paper.authors[0] or "") if paper.authors else ""
    surname = "".join(c for c in surname.lower() if c.isalnum())
    year = "".join(c for c in str(paper.year or "") if c.isalnum())
    return f"{surname or 'unknown'}{year or 'nd'}"
@

Two papers by different Smiths in the same year need different keys.
The first one keeps the plain key and later ones get a letter suffix,
[[a]] for the first clash, then [[b]], and after [[z]] two letters, so
the suffix never runs into the characters after [[z]] that biber would
reject. Suffixes are assigned in list order, and a key depends only on
the papers before it: appending papers can suffix the new ones but
never changes a key already handed out. The synthesis relies on that
to extend its key space without invalidating cached prose
(\cref{sec:synthesis-key-space} in the LLM review module). Should a
suffixed key happen to equal a key some other paper produced plainly,
the next suffix is taken.

<<review report generation>>=
def collision_suffix(clash: int) -> str:
    """Return the suffix for the `clash`th clash: a, ..., z, aa, ab, ..."""
    letters = ""
    while clash > 0:
        clash, rest = divmod(clash - 1, 26)
        letters = chr(ord("a") + rest) + letters
    return letters


def assign_cite_keys(papers: list[Paper]) -> list[str]:
    """Return a unique citation key for each of `papers`, in order."""
    keys: list[str] = []
    used: set[str] = set()
    clashes: dict[str, int] = {}
    for paper in papers:
        base = key = cite_key_base(paper)
        while key in used:
            clashes[base] = clashes.get(base, 0) + 1
            key = base + collision_suffix(clashes[base])
        used.add(key)
        keys.append(key)
    return keys
@

Let's check the surname forms, the sanitiser and the fallbacks, and
that suffixes start at [[a]] and leave earlier keys alone:

<<test functions>>=
class TestCiteKeys:
    def test_author_surname(self):
        assert author_surname("John Smith") == "Smith"
        assert author_surname("Smith") == "Smith"
        assert author_surname("Smith, John") == "Smith"
        assert author_surname("van Gogh, Vincent") == "van Gogh"
        assert author_surname("  ") == ""

    def test_key_base(self):
        def key(authors, year=2024):
            return cite_key_base(Paper(title="T", authors=authors, year=year))

        assert key(["Smith, John"]) == "smith2024"
        assert key(["Ann O'Brien"]) == "obrien2024"
        assert key(["Bo Smith-Jones"]) == "smithjones2024"
        assert key(["van Gogh, Vincent"]) == "vangogh2024"
        assert key(["Jörg Müller"]) == "müller2024"
        assert key(["Smith"], year=None) == "smithnd"
        assert key([]) == "unknown2024"
        assert key([""]) == "unknown2024"

    def test_suffixes_start_at_a_and_are_stable(self):
        smiths = [
            Paper(title=str(i), authors=[f"A{i} Smith"], year=2024)
            for i in range(29)
        ]
        keys = assign_cite_keys(smiths)
        assert keys[:3] == ["smith2024", "smith2024a", "smith2024b"]
        assert keys[26:] == ["smith2024z", "smith2024aa", "smith2024ab"]
        assert assign_cite_keys(smiths[:2]) == keys[:2]
        assert len(set(keys)) == len(keys)
@

The report keys its map by [[id(decision)]] rather than by paper id.
[[load_session]] can hand back two decisions that resolve to the same
paper id (it derives arXiv DOIs from URLs at load time, so two entries
that were distinct when saved may coincide afterwards), and the report
lists both; keyed by object identity they get two keys and two entries,
whereas a paper-id map would silently drop one and biber would then
reject the duplicate entry key.

<<review report generation>>=
def report_cite_keys(decisions: list[ReviewDecision]) -> dict[int, str]:
    """Map `id(decision)` to a unique citation key for each decision."""
    keys = assign_cite_keys([d.paper for d in decisions])
    return {id(d): key for d, key in zip(decisions, keys)}
@

The synthesis resolves the other way, by paper id, after collapsing
such duplicates onto their first occurrence, because its cached prose
cites a paper, not a record. The two callers therefore share the
scheme but not the identity a key is looked up by, and that is why the
shared \LaTeX{} builders below still take a [[cite_key]] resolver
instead of a key map.

Keying by object identity is what keeps two decisions for one paper
apart, and a comma-form author now yields the surname:

<<test functions>>=
class TestReportCiteKeys:
    def test_same_paper_twice_gets_distinct_keys(self):
        paper = Paper(title="Twice", authors=["Smith, Ann"], year=2024)
        decisions = [
            ReviewDecision(paper=paper, provider="a",
                           status=DecisionStatus.KEPT),
            ReviewDecision(paper=paper, provider="b",
                           status=DecisionStatus.DISCARDED),
        ]
        keys = report_cite_keys(decisions)
        assert keys[id(decisions[0])] == "smith2024"
        assert keys[id(decisions[1])] == "smith2024a"
