\paragraph{Exclusion criteria and other versions.}
A row that sorts among the \enquote{other} rows is there for one of
several reasons, and the caption must not give them all one name
(issue~\#169). The note tags fall in two groups: most say that the
record is \emph{another record of a cited source}---a duplicate, a
reprint, a report or thesis version, an erratum---and the caption's
\enquote{duplicates or other versions of cited sources} is true of
exactly those; the remaining two, [[recorded-not-cited]] and
[[no-abstract-title-only]], say nothing about another version.
[[AUDIT_VERSION_NOTES]] names the first group.

A discard that carries neither a category nor a note tag is a third
kind. A systematic review states its exclusion criteria as labelled
tags and discards each record with the label of the criterion it
failed---[[no-abstract]] for a record with no abstract to screen,
[[new-scheme]] or whatever the review's method defines---so the tag
\emph{is} the reason for the exclusion. Scholar cannot know a review's
criteria in advance: they are the author's own words, written into the
research context and passed to [[llm classify -t]] and
[[sessions decide --discard -t]]. What identifies a criterion is
therefore its place in the decision, not a fixed list: a discarded
decision without a category and without a note tag, and its tag that
is not one of the themes the caller named.  When there are several,
the \emph{last} is the criterion, for the reason the last category tag
is the decision ([[audit_category]]): [[sessions decide -t]] appends,
so a record first discarded as [[usable-security]] and then, on a second
look, as [[no-abstract]] carries both, and the second word is the
reviewer's latest.  The row's reason column shows that tag alone, also
when [[--theme]] is given (which otherwise lists every tag that is not
a theme as a note), so the row and the caption's count name the same
criterion. Counting such
records as duplicates told the reader of an appendix that, say, a
hundred records without abstracts were versions of cited papers, which
is a false statement about the review: one review held 1\,483 records
discarded as [[no-abstract]], and its caption called nearly all of them
duplicates, of which about 26 were. The caption counts each criterion
on its own, under its own label, which is also the reason the row
shows, so the reader can match the count to the rows. One criterion has
a sentence of its own: [[no-abstract]], the criterion the
backing-claims method puts on every review's list, reads \enquote{excluded
for lack of an abstract} (localised in [[criteria]]); any other
criterion is named by its label, since Scholar has no words for the
author's own criteria.

A discard that carries a note as well (a duplicate record that also has
no abstract) is a note row: its reason column shows the note, so that is
what the caption counts it under. Whatever is left among the
\enquote{other} rows---a [[recorded-not-cited]] note, a machine keep
under a theme, which is not a citation---is counted plainly as
\enquote{other hits}, so that the caption's counts still add up to the
number of unique hits.

