Because the category is read from the tags, a decision that overturns
an earlier one must not leave the earlier category behind
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/177}).  When a record's
status changes, [[sessions decide]] removes the old category tags,
since the new decision brings its own, and keeps every other tag.
Removing more was tried and lost data: dropping a discarded record's
motivations along with its category also dropped the themes of a paper
that had been kept, discarded and kept again, and a theme is the
author's own reading of the paper.  A motivation left on a re-kept
paper does less harm: the cited rule reads the status first, so the
paper is cited all the same, and at worst the reason column names the
leftover tag, which the author can remove.

The status is not the only way a record gets a second category.  An
agent that re-decides a kept record with another category
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/195}) leaves the status
as it was, so the rule above removed nothing, and the record held two
categories of which the audit table, reading the last, showed one.  A
decision that brings a category of its own therefore also replaces the
old one, whatever the status, and a decision may bring only one:
[[sessions decide]] refuses several category [[--tag]]s before it
touches the session.  Themes and motivations are still kept, for the
reason above.  What the rules cannot do is remove a tag the author no
longer wants, such as an exclusion criterion that a re-kept paper
carries and the cited rule then reads as a theme; [[--untag]] removes
named tags, before the new tags are added.

<<review report generation>>=
def tags_after_status_change(tags: list[str]) -> list[str]:
    """Return the tags a record keeps when a new decision changes its status.

    Only the category tags (`AUDIT_CATEGORIES`) go; themes, motivations
    and notes stay.
    """
    return [t for t in tags if t not in AUDIT_CATEGORIES]


def tags_after_decision(
    tags: Sequence[str],
    status_changed: bool,
    new_tags: Sequence[str] = (),
    removed_tags: Sequence[str] = (),
) -> list[str]:
    """Return a record's tags after `sessions decide` (before it adds).

    The category tags go when the status changed or when `new_tags`
    brings a category of its own (a record holds one category);
    `removed_tags` go whatever they are. The rest stays.
    """
    if status_changed or any(t in AUDIT_CATEGORIES for t in new_tags):
        tags = tags_after_status_change(tags)
    return [t for t in tags if t not in removed_tags]
@

[[tags_after_decision]] is the one place that says which tags a decision
leaves behind, so [[sessions decide]] has no tag logic of its own beyond
calling it.  The category tags are dropped if the status changed (the
rule above) or if the decision names a category itself, since the new
category then stands in for the old one; [[removed_tags]] are removed
whatever they are, categories included, and a name the record does not
hold is ignored here (the command reports it).  The tags the decision
adds come afterwards, from [[add_tag]], so a tag given to both options
ends up on the record.

