The same paper often arrives from several providers, sometimes with and
sometimes without a DOI, so a session may hold it more than once. The
audit table is about works, not records: rows are merged on a work key,
the providers are joined, and the best-placed decision wins---a cited
copy over a category, a category over a note---because the paper is
what it is whatever the other providers' copies were classified as.
The row's notes are the exception: they are united from all copies
(\cref{sec:audit-row-notes}). The
key is the title reduced to its letters and digits, case-folded, plus
the first author's surname treated the same way. Punctuation, quotes
and capitalisation differ between providers for the same work, and the
year is left out on purpose: a preprint and its journal article, or
OpenAlex's DOI-registration year against the publication year, are the
same work to a reader of the appendix, and counting them twice inflated
the numbers the appendices quoted. The surname is found by
[[author_surname]], the rule the citation keys use
(\cref{sec:cite-keys}). The key used to split the name itself, and a
first author that is blank or only whitespace, which some provider
records have, split to no words at all and raised an [[IndexError]]
that aborted the whole table (issue~\#122); [[author_surname]] returns
an empty surname instead, so such a record merges on its title alone.

Titles are kept whole by default: the table exists so a reader can look
a hit up, and a title cut at 180 characters is one that cannot be
found. A caller with a narrow column can ask for truncation, which
then cuts at a word boundary before escaping, so the cut cannot land
inside an escape sequence, and marks the cut with an ellipsis.

A decision's [[provider]] is itself a joined string when the session
merged records at search time (\enquote{openalex; dblp}), so providers
are split on the separators the session uses, mapped to their codes
and deduplicated before they are joined again for the cell; an
unmapped name is kept as written.  The row takes only the names the
decision's evidence supports ([[decision_provider_evidence]],
\cref{sec:provider-evidence}), since an old merged search wrote every
merged provider's name on every paper.  Every registered provider has a
code and a full name, the source-pinned preprint servers included, so
that case only arises for a provider string from outside Scholar.  The
servers are named as they style themselves (\enquote{bioRxiv},
\enquote{Preprints.org}); without entries the appendix would print the
registry's lower-case key, \enquote{researchsquare}, which no reader
can look up.

<<review report generation>>=
def provider_short_names(provider: str | None) -> list[str]:
    """Split a decision's provider string into deduplicated codes."""
    names: list[str] = []
    for part in PROVIDER_SEPARATORS.split(provider or ""):
        part = part.strip()
        if not part:
            continue
        short = PROVIDER_SHORT_NAMES.get(part.lower(), part)
        if short not in names:
            names.append(short)
    return names


def audit_work_key(paper: Paper) -> tuple[str, str]:
    """Reduce a paper to the key its audit row is merged on."""
    def letters(text: str) -> str:
        cleaned = clean_metadata_text(text or "")
        folded = unicodedata.normalize("NFKD", cleaned).casefold()
        return "".join(c for c in folded if c.isalnum())

    first = (paper.authors[0] or "") if paper.authors else ""
    return letters(paper.title), letters(author_surname(first))


def shorten_title(title: str, limit: int | None) -> str:
    """Cut `title` at a word boundary within `limit`, marking the cut."""
    if limit is None or len(title) <= limit:
        return title
    cut = title[:limit].rsplit(" ", 1)[0] or title[:limit]
    return cut.rstrip() + "\u2026"


def collect_audit_rows(
    session: ReviewSession,
    *,
    theme_names: dict[str, str],
    strings: dict[str, str],
    title_limit: int | None = None,
) -> list[AuditRow]:
    """Merge the session's decisions into sorted audit rows."""
    rows: dict[tuple[str, str], AuditRow] = {}
    copies: dict[tuple[str, str], list[ReviewDecision]] = {}
    best: dict[tuple[str, str], ReviewDecision] = {}
    for decision in session.decisions:
        paper = decision.paper
        title = paper.title or ""
        year = str(paper.year) if paper.year else ""
        key = audit_work_key(paper)
        providers = provider_short_names(
            ", ".join(decision_provider_evidence(decision))
        )
        reason, order = audit_reason(
            decision, theme_names=theme_names, strings=strings
        )
        excluded = audit_excluded(decision)
        criterion = audit_exclusion_criterion(
            decision, theme_names=theme_names, notes=strings["notes"]
        )
        version = audit_version_note(decision)
        copies.setdefault(key, []).append(decision)
        row = rows.get(key)
        if row is None:
            best[key] = decision
            rows[key] = AuditRow(
                title=escape_latex(shorten_title(title, title_limit)),
                year=year,
                providers=providers,
                status=decision.status,
                reason=reason,
                order=order,
                excluded=excluded,
                criterion=criterion,
                version=version,
            )
            continue
        for provider in providers:
            if provider not in row.providers:
                row.providers.append(provider)
        if not row.year:
            row.year = year
        if order < row.order:
            row.status, row.reason, row.order = decision.status, reason, order
            row.excluded, row.criterion = excluded, criterion
            row.version = version
            best[key] = decision
    for key, row in rows.items():
        <<unite the notes of the row's copies>>
    return sorted(rows.values(), key=lambda r: (r.order, r.title.lower()))
