\paragraph{The notes of a work's copies}
\label{sec:audit-row-notes}

The best-placed copy used to give its row everything, notes included,
and the notes of the other copies were lost
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/196}).  One review
tagged the DOI copy of four cited works [[cited-in-report]]; in each, a
copy confirmed from the classifier and placed as well came first,
represented the row, and the note vanished from the appendix, which
then contradicted the review.  The only workaround was to tag every
copy, and nothing told the author so.

A note records a fact about the work---that it is cited in the report,
that it is another version of a cited source, that it was judged on its
title---and that fact holds whichever copy represents the row.  The
category, the status and the confidence are different: each is the
judgement of one record, a row can show only one, and the best-placed
copy's is the one shown, as before.  So the row keeps the best copy's
reason and place and adds the notes of the other copies.  The themes are
not united, although they are tags as well: a theme is read as the
reason a work is cited, so a theme lent by a discarded copy, or by a
machine's keep, would make the row look like a citation the review
never made.  A tag outside Scholar's note vocabulary, such as
[[cited-in-report]], is a note only under [[--theme]]: without it, any
tag that is neither a category nor a note is a theme, and stays with its
copy.

Each copy lends exactly the notes its own row would show
([[audit_note_tags]]): a pending copy lends its notes like any other,
and a discard under an exclusion criterion lends nothing, since its
other tags are the earlier criteria the reviewer replaced, not notes.
The best copy's own
notes come first and the lent ones follow in the order of the
decisions, each once, so a note on two copies is written once and the
row reads the same on every export of the session.

The best copy is known only once every copy has been seen, so the notes
are united after the merge loop, and only a row whose other copies lend
a note changes.  Its reason is computed again by [[audit_reason]] with
the lent notes, which keeps one function deciding how a reason is
written: the lent notes go in parentheses after the label, also after
the pending label (after the pending copy's own notes) and after an
exclusion criterion, and join the notes
of a row whose reason is its notes.  The row's order and status are
not touched, so a note never moves a work between the caption's
categories: a cited row with a lent note is still cited.

<<unite the notes of the row's copies>>=
lent = [
    tag
    for copy in copies[key] if copy is not best[key]
    for tag in audit_note_tags(
        copy, theme_names=theme_names, notes=strings["notes"]
    )
]
if lent:
    row.reason, _ = audit_reason(
        best[key], theme_names=theme_names, strings=strings,
        also_notes=lent,
    )
    <<count a lent version note>>
<<add the notes of the work's other copies>>=
note_texts += [
    audit_note_text(tag, notes)
    for tag in dict.fromkeys(also_notes) if tag not in own_notes
]
@

The caption counts the \enquote{other} rows by what their reason column
shows (\cref{sec:audit-table}), and a row noted as another version of a
cited source counts among the duplicates.  A lent version note is shown
like the row's own, so it makes the row a version row too: a machine's
keep under a theme, which alone would be one of the \enquote{other
hits}, counts as a duplicate once a copy of it is noted as one.  A row
under an exclusion criterion is still counted under its criterion, the
reason its column leads with, as the caption already counts it.

<<count a lent version note>>=
row.version = row.version or any(
    tag in AUDIT_VERSION_NOTES for tag in lent
)
@

The first case is the one reported in issue~\#196: two copies placed
equally, the first (and so the row's) confirmed from the classifier,
the second, the DOI copy, carrying the reviewer's own note, with the
themes named.  The second lends a note from Scholar's vocabulary,
which needs no [[--theme]].  The others pin the order of the notes,
what must \emph{not} travel---a theme, an exclusion criterion and the
criterion it replaced---where lent notes go after a criterion, after
the pending label of an untagged machine keep and on a pending copy,
and that the row's place, and the caption's count, follow the rules
above.

