The caption also says when the databases were searched: the range of
the dates on which they answered ([[search_source_date]]), as PRISMA
asks for the date each source was last searched
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/167}).  A cached answer
counts on the date it was fetched, not on the day of the run that
served it.  Searches whose date is unknown---records from before dates
were kept, cached answers whose fetch date the cache does not know---are
counted in a sentence of their own rather than silently left out of
the range, and a session with no dates at all gets no sentence, as
before.

<<review report generation>>=
def audit_dates_sentence(
    strings: dict, records: list[SearchRecord] | None
) -> str:
    """Say when the searches of `records` were answered; "" if never known."""
    records = records or []
    dates = [search_source_date(r) for r in answered_records(records)]
    known = sorted(d for d in dates if d)
    if not known:
        return ""
    if known[0] == known[-1]:
        sentence = strings["searched_on"].format(start=known[0])
    else:
        sentence = strings["searched_range"].format(
            start=known[0], end=known[-1]
        )
    unknown = len(dates) - len(known)
    if unknown:
        noun = strings["search"] if len(dates) == 1 else strings["searches"]
        sentence += " " + strings["dates_unknown"].format(
            n=unknown, total=len(dates), searches=noun
        )
    return sentence
@

