\subsection{Checking a semantic search against sessions}
\label{sec:semantic-check}

The record of a keyword search shows what was found and cannot show
what was missed.  A semantic search (\cref{sec:ranked-list}) can serve
as a check on it: ask the question in plain words, and look up every
work it returns in the review's sessions.  A returned work that bears
on the question but is in no session is a gap in the keyword queries.
[[scholar sessions check]] (\cref{sec:sessions-check} in the CLI
chapter) runs the search; this subsection holds the lookup, which
needs no network and is tested on its own.

\paragraph{The index.}
A work is looked up in three ways, because a session and a semantic
search may hold the same work under different identities:
\begin{description}
\item[By identity] [[Paper.id]], the DOI where there is one (normalised
  as [[Paper]] does: lower case, no resolver prefix).
\item[By version links] the DOIs in a decision's [[preprint_versions]]
  and [[published_versions]] (\cref{sec:version-dedup} in the scholar
  module): when the session adopted a preprint, or collapsed one onto
  its published version, the other DOI still names the same work.
\item[By title and first author] [[audit_work_key]], the key the audit
  table merges its rows on (\cref{sec:audit-table}), so a work the
  sessions hold without a DOI is still found.  Only when its title part
  is not empty: two works without a title would otherwise match each
  other on the author alone.  A session paper recorded without authors
  is also indexed under its title alone, since its title-and-author key
  could never equal that of a returned work with authors.
\end{description}
The same holds for the identity of a work without a DOI: its
[[Paper.id]] is a hash of title and first author, so an untitled work
is not indexed under it either.
A match records how it was made: [[doi]] for the first two,
[[title+author]] for the third (and for a DOI-less [[Paper.id]], which
is itself a hash of title and author), [[title]] for a title alone.
A session paper without authors records [[title]] on all three title
keys: its title-and-author key, and the hash behind its DOI-less
[[Paper.id]], hold an empty author, so a work that matches them has
matched on the title alone.

<<review report generation>>=
CHECK_BY_DOI = "doi"
CHECK_BY_TITLE = "title+author"
CHECK_BY_TITLE_ONLY = "title"


def _identity_key(paper: Paper) -> str | None:
    """`Paper.id`, unless it is the hash of an empty title."""
    if paper.doi or (paper.title or "").strip():
        return paper.id
    return None


def _work_index_key(paper: Paper) -> str | None:
    """The title-and-author key of `paper`, or None without a title."""
    title, surname = audit_work_key(paper)
    return f"work:{title}|{surname}" if title else None


def _title_index_key(paper: Paper) -> str | None:
    """The title-only key of `paper`, or None without a title."""
    title, _ = audit_work_key(paper)
    return f"title:{title}" if title else None


def session_match_index(
    sessions: list[ReviewSession],
) -> dict[str, list[tuple[str, ReviewDecision, str]]]:
    """Index every decision of `sessions` by the keys a work matches on.

    Each key maps to `(session name, decision, matched_by)` entries;
    `matched_by` is `"doi"`, `"title+author"` or `"title"`.
    """
    index: dict[str, list[tuple[str, ReviewDecision, str]]] = {}

    def add(key: str | None, entry: tuple[str, ReviewDecision, str]) -> None:
        if not key:
            return
        entries = index.setdefault(key, [])
        if not any(e[0] == entry[0] and e[1] is entry[1] for e in entries):
            entries.append(entry)

    for session in sessions:
        name = session.name or session.query
        for decision in session.decisions:
            paper = decision.paper
            authorless = not any(
                (author or "").strip() for author in paper.authors
            )
            by_title = CHECK_BY_TITLE_ONLY if authorless else CHECK_BY_TITLE
            by_id = CHECK_BY_DOI if paper.doi else by_title
            add(_identity_key(paper), (name, decision, by_id))
            for key in version_link_dois(paper):
                add(key, (name, decision, CHECK_BY_DOI))
            add(_work_index_key(paper), (name, decision, by_title))
            if authorless:
                add(_title_index_key(paper),
                    (name, decision, CHECK_BY_TITLE_ONLY))
    return index
@

\paragraph{When a title match is not the same work.}
Title and first author's surname is a weak key.  Short generic titles
(\enquote{Editorial}, \enquote{Introduction}) by authors with common
surnames collide, and a search by meaning returns exactly such
near-namesakes.  Calling one of them \enquote{in the session} would hide
the gap the check is looking for.  So a title match is dropped when the
two records say outright that they are different works: two DOIs that
differ and are not linked as versions, or years more than one apart.
That rule, [[plausibly_same_work]], lives with deduplication in the
scholar module (\cref{sec:same-work-guard}), which asks the same
question when it decides whether two records are one; the version-link
DOIs that the index above adds come from [[version_link_dois]] there.
A DOI match needs no guard: the DOI is the identity.

<<review imports>>=
from scholar.scholar import plausibly_same_work, version_link_dois
@

\paragraph{Buckets.}
Each match gets a bucket from the decision, in the audit table's terms
(\cref{sec:audit-table}).  A paper the audit table would list as
\emph{cited}---a human keep with theme tags or no tags at all---is
\emph{bearing}; so is a paper classified as supporting or qualifying
the claim.  The cited rule is not derived again here: [[audit_reason]]
decides it, so the check and the appendix cannot disagree about which
papers a review cites.  An LLM keep without a category is a candidate,
not a citation, and stays \emph{unclassified}.  \emph{Adjacent} and
\emph{other topic} are the two categories that do not bear on the
claim, and \emph{unclassified} is any other decision without a
category, pending ones included.  As in the audit table, the status
wins over the category (issue~\#89): a discarded paper is never
bearing, whatever its category says; it is \emph{excluded}, and so is
a discarded paper without a category.  An adjacent or off-topic paper
is discarded as a rule, so its category already says why and stays its
bucket.

A work in several sessions, or several times in one, takes the most
relevant bucket any of its matches has, in the order of
[[CHECK_BUCKETS]]: bearing, adjacent, unclassified, other topic,
excluded.  The question the check answers is whether the review has
the work in view, so one session that counts it as bearing outweighs
another that discarded it for its own, narrower question, and a
pending decision, which someone still has to make, outweighs a
discard.  The per-session list shows every session's view.  A work
without any match is \emph{not in any session}, the bucket the check
is for.

<<review report generation>>=
CHECK_BEARING = "bearing"
CHECK_NOT_IN_SESSIONS = "not in any session"
CHECK_BUCKETS = (
    CHECK_BEARING, "adjacent", "unclassified", "other topic", "excluded",
    CHECK_NOT_IN_SESSIONS,
)
CHECK_CATEGORY_BUCKETS = {
    "supports-claim": CHECK_BEARING,
    "qualifies-claim": CHECK_BEARING,
    "adjacent-subtopic": "adjacent",
    "off-topic-false-hit": "other topic",
}


def check_bucket(decision: ReviewDecision) -> str:
    """The check bucket of one session decision."""
    _, order = audit_reason(
        decision, theme_names={}, strings=AUDIT_STRINGS["en"]
    )
    if order == AUDIT_ORDER_CITED:
        return CHECK_BEARING
    category = audit_category(decision.tags)
    discarded = decision.status == DecisionStatus.DISCARDED
    bucket = CHECK_CATEGORY_BUCKETS.get(category, "unclassified")
    if discarded and bucket in (CHECK_BEARING, "unclassified"):
        return "excluded"
    return bucket


def work_bucket(matches: list["SessionMatch"]) -> str:
    """The most relevant bucket (first in `CHECK_BUCKETS`) of `matches`."""
    found = {match.bucket for match in matches}
    return next(
        (bucket for bucket in CHECK_BUCKETS if bucket in found),
        CHECK_NOT_IN_SESSIONS,
    )
@

\paragraph{Matching the returned works.}
A returned work collects the matches of all its keys, once per session
decision, and drops the title matches that the guards above rule out.
When one decision is found both by DOI and by title, the match says
[[doi]], the stronger of the two.  The category shown is the deciding
audit category, or else the last tag, which is the reviewer's latest
word on the paper.

<<review report generation>>=
@dataclass
class SessionMatch:
    """How one session classified a work returned by a check."""

    session: str
    status: str
    category: str | None
    matched_by: str
    bucket: str


@dataclass
class CheckRow:
    """One work returned by a semantic check, with its session matches."""

    rank: int
    title: str
    year: int | None
    doi: str | None
    paper_id: str
    bucket: str
    matches: list[SessionMatch]


@dataclass
class KnownRow:
    """Where a known study ended up: its rank, or None if not returned."""

    known: str
    rank: int | None
    title: str | None
    doi: str | None
    bucket: str
    matches: list[SessionMatch]


def _session_matches(
    entries: list[tuple[str, ReviewDecision, str]],
) -> list[SessionMatch]:
    """One match per session decision, preferring a DOI match."""
    best: dict[tuple[str, int], tuple[str, ReviewDecision, str]] = {}
    for entry in entries:
        key = (entry[0], id(entry[1]))
        if key not in best or entry[2] == CHECK_BY_DOI:
            best[key] = entry
    return [
        SessionMatch(
            session=name,
            status=decision.status.value,
            category=audit_category(decision.tags)
            or (decision.tags[-1] if decision.tags else None),
            matched_by=matched_by,
            bucket=check_bucket(decision),
        )
        for name, decision, matched_by in best.values()
    ]


def match_work(
    paper: Paper, index: dict[str, list[tuple[str, ReviewDecision, str]]]
) -> list[SessionMatch]:
    """Look `paper` up in a `session_match_index`."""
    keys = [_identity_key(paper), _work_index_key(paper),
            _title_index_key(paper)]
    keys += version_link_dois(paper)
    entries = [
        entry for key in keys if key for entry in index.get(key, [])
        if entry[2] == CHECK_BY_DOI
        or plausibly_same_work(paper, entry[1].paper)
    ]
    return _session_matches(entries)
@

\paragraph{Known studies.}
A check is often run because one study is known to be relevant---the
issue's was cited in another tool's report---and the question is
whether the search finds it, and where.  A known study is given by its
DOI or by a part of its title.  A DOI is compared exactly (after
normalisation); anything that does not normalise to a DOI is a title
part, matched case-insensitively, as the earlier script did.  A known
study the search did not return is still looked up in the sessions:
whether the review has it is the other half of the answer.

<<review report generation>>=
def _known_doi(known: str) -> str | None:
    """The normalised DOI if `known` is one, else None."""
    doi = Paper._normalize_doi(known)
    return doi if doi.startswith("10.") else None


def locate_known(
    known: str,
    rows: list[CheckRow],
    sessions: list[ReviewSession],
    index: dict[str, list[tuple[str, ReviewDecision, str]]],
) -> KnownRow:
    """Find a known study among the returned rows and in the sessions."""
    doi = _known_doi(known)
    part = known.casefold()

    def is_known(title: str | None, row_doi: str | None) -> bool:
        if doi:
            return bool(row_doi) and Paper._normalize_doi(row_doi) == doi
        return part in (title or "").casefold()

    for row in rows:
        if is_known(row.title, row.doi):
            return KnownRow(known, row.rank, row.title, row.doi,
                            row.bucket, row.matches)
    if doi:
        matches = _session_matches(index.get(f"doi:{doi}", []))
    else:
        matches = _session_matches([
            (session.name or session.query, decision, CHECK_BY_TITLE_ONLY)
            for session in sessions for decision in session.decisions
            if is_known(decision.paper.title, decision.paper.doi)
        ])
    return KnownRow(known, None, None, doi, work_bucket(matches), matches)
@

\paragraph{The check.}
[[check_against_sessions]] puts the parts together.  It reads the
sessions and writes nothing: the check is evidence about the review,
and running it must not change the record it is evidence about.
[[check_tally]] counts the rows per bucket, in bucket order, for the
summary line of each question.

<<review report generation>>=
def check_against_sessions(
    papers: list[Paper],
    sessions: list[ReviewSession],
    known: list[str] | tuple[str, ...] = (),
) -> tuple[list[CheckRow], list[KnownRow]]:
    """Classify each returned paper by the sessions; locate known studies.

    `papers` are in rank order.  Returns one `CheckRow` per paper and
    one `KnownRow` per entry of `known` (a DOI or a title part).
    """
    index = session_match_index(sessions)
    rows = []
    for rank, paper in enumerate(papers, start=1):
        matches = match_work(paper, index)
        rows.append(CheckRow(
            rank=rank,
            title=paper.title or "",
            year=paper.year,
            doi=paper.doi,
            paper_id=paper.id,
            bucket=work_bucket(matches),
            matches=matches,
        ))
    known_rows = [locate_known(k, rows, sessions, index) for k in known]
    return rows, known_rows


def check_tally(rows: list[CheckRow]) -> dict[str, int]:
    """Count `rows` per bucket, in `CHECK_BUCKETS` order, nonzero only."""
    counts = {bucket: 0 for bucket in CHECK_BUCKETS}
    for row in rows:
        counts[row.bucket] += 1
    return {bucket: n for bucket, n in counts.items() if n}
@

